const COMBINED_QUESTIONS = [{"id":"ml-1","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"A maker wants to describe a business need in plain language and have Power Apps generate both an app and a data model with no coding. Which capability should they use?","options":["Copilot in Power Apps","A managed solution import","A Dataverse rollup column","The classic solution explorer"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1A","topic":"Copilot in Power Apps","sub":null,"explanation":"Copilot in Power Apps lets you build apps using natural language: you describe your business needs and the service generates an app and a data model for you, with no coding required. Solutions, rollup columns, and the solution explorer don't generate apps from a description.","source":{"title":"Copilot in Power Apps overview","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/canvas-apps/ai-overview"}},{"id":"ml-2","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Microsoft 365 Copilot for model-driven apps lets users gain insights into their app data through natural-language conversations. What must an administrator do before users can see it?","options":["Convert the app to a canvas app","Enable Microsoft 365 Copilot for the environment / app before it becomes visible","Add a business process flow to every table","Publish the app as a managed solution"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1A","topic":"Microsoft 365 Copilot in apps","sub":null,"explanation":"An administrator must enable Microsoft 365 Copilot in the application (at the environment and/or app level) before it becomes visible. Users also need both a Power Apps premium license and a Microsoft 365 Copilot license to use it.","source":{"title":"Add Microsoft 365 Copilot for app users in model-driven apps","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/model-driven-apps/add-microsoft-365-copilot"}},{"id":"ml-3","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which Power Platform environment type is a single-user space that can't be used to run or share production apps and is intended for building, testing, and learning?","options":["Production","Sandbox","Developer","Default"],"correct":[2],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1A","topic":"Environment Types","sub":null,"explanation":"A Developer environment (formerly Community) is a single-user environment that can't run or share production apps; it's meant for building, testing, and learning with Power Apps, Power Automate, and Dataverse. Production runs apps for intended use, Sandbox is for safe non-production dev/test, and Default is auto-created and shared by all tenant users.","source":{"title":"ALM basics with Microsoft Power Platform — Environments","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-platform/alm/basics-alm"}},{"id":"ml-4","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Following healthy ALM, which statement about deploying to a production environment is correct?","options":["Unmanaged solutions should be deployed straight to production so they can be edited there","Managed solutions are deployed to non-development environments such as test and production; components can't be edited directly in a managed solution","Managed and unmanaged solutions behave identically once imported","A managed solution can be imported into the same environment that holds its originating unmanaged solution"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1A","topic":"Managed vs Unmanaged Solutions","sub":null,"explanation":"Managed solutions are deployed to any environment that isn't a development environment for that solution (test, UAT, production). You can't edit components directly within a managed solution, and you can't import a managed solution into the same environment that contains its originating unmanaged solution. Unmanaged solutions are the development source.","source":{"title":"Solution concepts — Managed and unmanaged solutions","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-platform/alm/solution-concepts-alm"}},{"id":"ml-5","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Why is it important to use a custom publisher (not the default) when you create solutions for an application?","options":["The publisher sets the customization prefix and is required to be consistent across solutions and environments","The publisher determines the timer length of cloud flows","Each publisher can only contain one table","The publisher encrypts business data inside the solution"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1A","topic":"Solution Publisher","sub":null,"explanation":"The solution publisher defines the customization prefix applied to new components, and Microsoft guidance is to use the same publisher for all your solutions across environments. Solutions never contain business data, and the publisher has nothing to do with flow timers or encryption.","source":{"title":"Organize your solutions — Solution publisher","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-platform/alm/organize-solutions"}},{"id":"ml-6","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"What is the key difference between standard tables and custom tables in Dataverse?","options":["Standard tables can't be customized in any way","Standard tables (such as User and Team) ship with Dataverse following Common Data Model best practices and can also be customized; custom tables are ones you create yourself","Custom tables can't store relationships","Standard tables are only available in canvas apps"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1B","topic":"Standard vs Custom Tables","sub":null,"explanation":"Standard tables (e.g., User, Team) are included with Dataverse, follow the Common Data Model, and can be customized (for example by adding columns). You can also create your own custom tables. Both support columns and relationships.","source":{"title":"Work with any data — Tables and columns","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/data-platform/work-with-any-data"}},{"id":"ml-7","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"A maker sets a column's requirement level to Business Required. What is the default app behavior?","options":["The column is hidden from all forms","Model-driven and canvas apps enforce the requirement, blocking save until the user enters a value (with options to override)","The value is calculated automatically","The column becomes read-only"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1B","topic":"Column Requirement Level","sub":null,"explanation":"Setting a column to Business Required means model-driven and canvas apps enforce it by default: the request isn't sent if the column has no value, and the user is prompted to enter data before saving. There are options to override this behavior if needed.","source":{"title":"Create and edit columns in Dataverse using Power Apps","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/data-platform/create-edit-field-portal"}},{"id":"ml-8","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which relationship type requires an intermediate (junction) relationship table, where deleting the relationship loses the connecting data?","options":["One-to-many (1:N)","Many-to-one (N:1)","Many-to-many (N:N)","Self-referential lookup"],"correct":[2],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1B","topic":"Relationship Types","sub":null,"explanation":"A many-to-many (N:N) relationship is implemented through an intermediate relationship table. Deleting the relationship deletes that relationship table and all the data connecting the two tables. 1:N and N:1 relationships are defined with a lookup column instead.","source":{"title":"Create N:N (many-to-many) table relationships in Dataverse","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/data-platform/create-edit-nn-relationships-solution-explorer"}},{"id":"ml-9","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"A maker adds a Lookup column to the related table in Power Apps. What does this create?","options":["A many-to-many relationship","A one-to-many / many-to-one relationship between the two tables","A calculated column","A new security role"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1B","topic":"Creating Relationships","sub":null,"explanation":"Creating a Lookup column is one of the ways to define a one-to-many (1:N) or many-to-one (N:1) relationship between two tables in Dataverse. A lookup on the 'many' side points to a single row on the 'one' side.","source":{"title":"Create one-to-many or many-to-one table relationships in Dataverse","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/data-platform/create-edit-1n-relationships"}},{"id":"ml-10","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"What does a Dataverse prompt column store?","options":["A static default value typed by the maker","Generative AI results produced from a natural-language prompt tied to other columns, persisted in the table","A link to an external SharePoint file","A list of security roles"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1B","topic":"Prompt Columns","sub":null,"explanation":"A prompt column is an AI-powered Dataverse data type. You define a natural-language prompt tied to input columns; the AI model generates a response that is stored persistently in the prompt column, enriching the record so it can be used in apps, flows, and reports.","source":{"title":"Prompt columns","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/data-platform/prompt-column"}},{"id":"ml-11","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"When are values in a Dataverse prompt column populated?","options":["Only when a user manually presses a Refresh button","When records are created and when the referenced input column values are updated","Once per year by a scheduled job","Only at solution import time"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1B","topic":"Prompt Column Behavior","sub":null,"explanation":"Prompt column values are populated when records are created and when the input column values referenced in the prompt definition are updated. Existing records aren't backfilled automatically when a prompt column is added to a table that already has rows.","source":{"title":"Specialized columns using code — Prompt columns","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/developer/data-platform/specialized-columns"}},{"id":"ml-12","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"A maker configures a row summary for an Account table. Where do these Copilot-generated summaries appear, and what controls their content?","options":["On all tables automatically, with fixed content","On the table's main forms and views, with content controlled by a custom prompt that specifies which columns to include","Only in Power BI reports","Only inside cloud flows"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1B","topic":"Copilot Row Summary","sub":null,"explanation":"A row summary is configured with a custom prompt that specifies which columns to include; once applied it appears in a consistent experience on the table's main forms (a collapsible summary bar) and from views. It gives users an at-a-glance view of the most important fields for a record.","source":{"title":"Configure a row summary for a model-driven app","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/data-platform/configure-form-row-summary"}},{"id":"ml-13","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"What is a Dataverse view?","options":["A query that defines which columns to display, how rows are sorted, and how rows are filtered for a table","A reusable canvas component","A row-level security rule","A type of cloud flow trigger"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1B","topic":"Dataverse Views","sub":null,"explanation":"A view is a type of query that displays specific rows and columns of data from a table — you specify which columns to show, the sort order, and the filter (row) criteria. Public views can then be surfaced in model-driven apps and lists.","source":{"title":"Create and modify Dataverse views by using the Data workspace","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-pages/configure/data-workspace-views"}},{"id":"ml-14","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"In the Dataverse role-based security model, how is a user's effective access to a record determined?","options":["By the single most restrictive security role assigned","By the least restrictive (accumulative) combination of all their security roles, team roles, business unit, and shared records","Only by the business unit, ignoring roles","By the order in which roles were assigned"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1B","topic":"Dataverse Security Model","sub":null,"explanation":"Dataverse security is accumulative: a user's access is the least restrictive (greatest) combination of all directly assigned security roles, roles inherited through teams, their business unit, and any records shared with them. Security roles are collections of privileges and access levels.","source":{"title":"Security concepts in Microsoft Dataverse","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-platform/admin/wp-security-cds"}},{"id":"ml-15","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"A specific column holds sensitive PII that only certain users should read. Which Dataverse feature provides this control?","options":["A business process flow","Column-level (field) security using a Column Security Profile","A rollup column","A managed solution layer"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1B","topic":"Column-Level Security","sub":null,"explanation":"Column-level security restricts Create/Read/Update access to an individual column. You enable it on the column and grant access through a Column Security Profile associated with users or teams. The user must already have record-level access; column security further narrows what they can see.","source":{"title":"Security concepts in Microsoft Dataverse — Column-level security","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-platform/admin/wp-security-cds"}},{"id":"ml-16","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which set of components is added to a model-driven app using the app designer?","options":["Screens, galleries, and text-input controls","Tables and their related tables, forms, views, charts, dashboards, and business process flows","Power BI datasets only","Sensitivity labels and DLP policies"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2A","topic":"Model-Driven App Components","sub":null,"explanation":"Model-driven app components include tables (and related tables), forms, columns, views, charts, dashboards, and business process flows, all assembled in the app designer. Screens, galleries, and text-input controls are canvas-app concepts.","source":{"title":"What are model-driven apps in Power Apps?","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/model-driven-apps/model-driven-app-overview"}},{"id":"ml-17","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"In the model-driven app designer, if no forms are selected for a table, what happens at runtime?","options":["The app fails to open","All forms for that table are displayed to users","Only the classic form is shown","The table is removed from the app"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2A","topic":"Adding Forms to an App","sub":null,"explanation":"If none of the forms for a table are selected in the app designer, all forms for that table are displayed to users (the same applies to views and charts). However, each table must have at least one active form for the app to run.","source":{"title":"Add or edit model-driven app components in the app designer","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/model-driven-apps/add-edit-app-components"}},{"id":"ml-18","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"How does a maker create a generative page in a model-driven app?","options":["By writing C# plug-in code","By selecting Add a page > Describe a page and describing the page in natural language, optionally linking Dataverse tables","By importing an Excel file","By configuring a DLP policy"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2A","topic":"Generative Pages","sub":null,"explanation":"In the app designer you select Add a page > Describe a page, then describe the page in natural language and link up to six Dataverse tables. The app agent generates the layout and React code, and you refine it conversationally.","source":{"title":"Generate a page using natural language","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/model-driven-apps/generative-pages"}},{"id":"ml-19","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"When building a generative page, how many Dataverse tables can a maker link to it?","options":["Exactly one","Up to six","Up to fifty","Unlimited"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2A","topic":"Generative Page Data","sub":null,"explanation":"When creating a generative page you can link up to six Dataverse tables via Add data > Add table. The agent uses those tables (and optionally attached images) to generate the page experience and logic.","source":{"title":"Generate a page using natural language — Add data","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/model-driven-apps/generative-pages"}},{"id":"ml-20","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"What is a key limit of a model-driven app dashboard?","options":["It can present data from up to six charts, grids, IFrames, or web resources at once","It can only display one chart","It cannot include any views","It can only be used in canvas apps"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2A","topic":"Dashboards","sub":null,"explanation":"A model-driven dashboard acts as a business-intelligence snapshot and can simultaneously present data from up to six charts, grids, IFrames, or web resources. Charts update automatically as users filter the underlying grid data.","source":{"title":"Customize visualizations and dashboards","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/developer/model-driven-apps/customize-visualizations-dashboards"}},{"id":"ml-21","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"How do charts behave relative to the grid (view) they are shown alongside in a model-driven app?","options":["They are static images that must be refreshed manually","They are integrated with the grid and update automatically to reflect filtering applied to the grid data","They can only show data from a different table than the grid","They require Power BI Premium"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2A","topic":"Charts in Model-Driven Apps","sub":null,"explanation":"Charts in model-driven apps are integrated with the grid and display data in context. They update automatically to reflect filtering done on the grid data, and drilling into the chart updates the corresponding grid.","source":{"title":"Customize visualizations and dashboards","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/developer/model-driven-apps/customize-visualizations-dashboards"}},{"id":"ml-22","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"A maker wants to quickly generate a lightweight responsive canvas app from an existing Dataverse table. Which option should they choose on the Power Apps home page?","options":["Start with data > Dataverse, select a table, then Create app","Create > Blank app > Tablet","Import a managed solution","Add a business process flow"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2B","topic":"Create Canvas App from Data","sub":null,"explanation":"The Start with data > Dataverse path lets you select an existing table and select Create app, generating a single-page gallery (responsive) app connected to that table. A blank app gives full layout control but isn't generated from data.","source":{"title":"Create a canvas app using Microsoft Dataverse","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/canvas-apps/data-platform-create-app-scratch"}},{"id":"ml-23","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"When Power Apps generates a canvas app from a Dataverse table, which three screens does it create?","options":["Login, Home, and Logout screens","Browse, Detail, and Edit screens","Chart, Dashboard, and Report screens","Trigger, Action, and Condition screens"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2B","topic":"Generated Canvas App Screens","sub":null,"explanation":"A generated canvas app includes a Browse screen (search/sort/select records), a Detail screen (read-only record details), and an Edit screen (form to create or edit records).","source":{"title":"How to create a canvas app connected to Dataverse from scratch","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/canvas-apps/how-to/build-connected-dataverse"}},{"id":"ml-24","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"The canvas app Accessibility checker flags that an interactive control has its accessible-label property empty. How is this issue classified?","options":["Tip","Warning","Error","Information"],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2B","topic":"Accessibility Checker","sub":null,"explanation":"A missing accessible label on an interactive control is classified as an Error, because it makes the app difficult or impossible to use for people who can't see the screen. Warnings and Tips are lower-severity classifications.","source":{"title":"Review a canvas app for accessibility in Power Apps","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/canvas-apps/accessibility-checker"}},{"id":"ml-25","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Why is replacing variable/collection initialization in App.OnStart with named formulas in App.Formulas recommended for performance?","options":["Named formulas run a flow on every keystroke","Each named formula is independent and evaluated only when its value is needed, so the app and Studio load faster","Named formulas disable error checking","Named formulas require a premium connector"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2B","topic":"Named Formulas","sub":null,"explanation":"Named formulas in App.Formulas are independent and evaluated only when their value is needed, so Power Apps Studio can analyze them separately and the first screen displays immediately. Microsoft reports load-time drops of up to 80% from this change. Named formulas are immutable, so use Set for mutable state.","source":{"title":"Build large and complex canvas apps — App.Formulas","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/canvas-apps/working-with-large-apps"}},{"id":"ml-26","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Where are user-defined functions (UDFs) defined in a canvas app?","options":["In the OnStart property of the first screen","In the App.Formulas property, using the FunctionName(Param:Type):OutputType = Formula syntax","In a separate Power Automate flow","In the Dataverse table designer"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2B","topic":"User-Defined Functions","sub":null,"explanation":"User-defined functions are defined alongside named formulas in the App.Formulas property using the syntax FunctionName(Parameter:DataType):OutputDataType = Formula. They let makers create reusable custom functions with parameters.","source":{"title":"Code optimization — User defined functions","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/guidance/coding-guidelines/code-optimization"}},{"id":"ml-27","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"What is the recommended way to reuse the same canvas component across multiple apps in an environment?","options":["Copy and paste the control between apps","Use a component library, which maintains dependencies and notifies makers of updates","Re-create the component in each app","Export the app as a managed solution every time"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2B","topic":"Component Libraries","sub":null,"explanation":"Component libraries are the recommended way to reuse components across apps. A consuming app maintains a dependency on the library component and the maker is alerted when updates are available. The older import-components-between-apps feature is retired.","source":{"title":"Component library","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/canvas-apps/component-library"}},{"id":"ml-28","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which canvas app property gives a global opportunity to intercept and handle an error anywhere in the app before the default error banner is shown?","options":["App.OnStart","App.OnError","Screen.OnVisible","Button.OnSelect"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2B","topic":"Global Error Handling","sub":null,"explanation":"App.OnError is evaluated whenever any formula in the app produces an error, providing a global place to intercept the error banner, log with Trace, or rethrow with the Error function. It controls error reporting only; it can't replace the error value the way IfError can.","source":{"title":"App object in Power Apps — OnError property","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-platform/power-fx/reference/object-app"}},{"id":"ml-29","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"A value must change while the app is running (for example, a counter the user updates). Which approach is appropriate?","options":["A named formula, because named formulas are mutable","A variable set with Set(), because named formulas are immutable","A rollup column","A component library"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2B","topic":"Variables vs Named Formulas","sub":null,"explanation":"Named formulas are immutable and can't be changed with Set. For mutable state that changes during the session, use a variable with the Set function. Named formulas are best for static values that don't change.","source":{"title":"Build large and complex canvas apps — App.Formulas vs App.OnStart","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/canvas-apps/working-with-large-apps"}},{"id":"ml-30","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"What is Power Apps Monitor primarily used for?","options":["Assigning security roles to users","Streaming and inspecting session events (data operations, control evaluations, errors, timing) to debug and diagnose performance","Designing dashboards","Creating business process flows"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2B","topic":"Monitor","sub":null,"explanation":"Monitor is a debugging tool that streams events from a session — operations like Select, Load Screen, Navigate, GetRows, with results, durations, and HTTP status codes (e.g., 404, 429) — to diagnose and troubleshoot performance and behavior issues in Studio or in a published app.","source":{"title":"Monitoring and testing — Monitor","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/guidance/coding-guidelines/monitoring-testing"}},{"id":"ml-31","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"To see the underlying formula for an event in Monitor while monitoring a published app, what must the maker do?","options":["Nothing — formulas always show by default","Enable the 'Debug published app' option in the app settings","Convert the app to a model-driven app","Remove all named formulas"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2B","topic":"Monitor on Published Apps","sub":null,"explanation":"By default the formula for an event is only shown during authoring. To show formulas for a published app in Monitor, the maker must turn on the 'Debug published app' option under File > Settings.","source":{"title":"Monitoring and testing — Monitor","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/guidance/coding-guidelines/monitoring-testing"}},{"id":"ml-32","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Agent builder in Power Apps lets a maker create an AI agent from an existing canvas app. What does it use to generate the agent's step-by-step process, knowledge, and triggers?","options":["A Power BI dataset","The app's metadata combined with the desired agent goal","A Dataverse rollup column","An imported managed solution"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2B","topic":"Agent Builder from an App","sub":null,"explanation":"Agent builder uses the app's metadata together with the desired agent goal to generate a step-by-step process, extract knowledge, and identify triggers, combining them with skills extracted from the app. The maker can then edit, test, and publish the agent in Microsoft Copilot Studio.","source":{"title":"Build an AI agent to automate your business process (agent builder)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/canvas-apps/agent-builder"}},{"id":"ml-33","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Where can a maker continue to edit, test, and publish an agent that was generated from a Power Apps canvas app?","options":["Microsoft Copilot Studio","The Exchange admin center","Power BI Desktop","Microsoft Purview"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2B","topic":"Create Agent from App","sub":null,"explanation":"After creating an agent from an app with agent builder, makers edit, test, and publish it in Microsoft Copilot Studio, which provides the full agent authoring and management experience.","source":{"title":"Build an AI agent to automate your business process (agent builder)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/canvas-apps/agent-builder"}},{"id":"ml-34","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"An invoice flow currently runs on every email received, but should only run for approved invoices. What is the most efficient way to prevent unnecessary runs?","options":["Add a Condition action after the trigger","Add a trigger condition so the flow only fires when the invoice is approved","Increase the timeout","Switch to a manual trigger"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3A","topic":"Trigger Conditions","sub":null,"explanation":"A trigger condition is evaluated before the flow runs, so the flow only fires when the condition is met (e.g., invoice approved). Unlike a Condition action — which runs after the flow already triggered and counts as an API request — trigger conditions avoid unnecessary runs and reduce request consumption.","source":{"title":"Customize your triggers with conditions","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-automate/customize-triggers"}},{"id":"ml-35","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"In the Dataverse 'When a row is added, modified, or deleted' trigger, which scope causes the flow to run when ANY user in the organization performs the action?","options":["User","Business Unit","Parent: Child business unit","Organization"],"correct":[3],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3A","topic":"Dataverse Trigger Scope","sub":null,"explanation":"The Organization scope triggers the flow when the action is taken by anyone within the organization or database. User scope triggers only for rows the flow owner owns; Business Unit and Parent:Child limit to a business unit and its children.","source":{"title":"Create a cloud flow with Microsoft Dataverse — scopes","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-automate/connection-cds"}},{"id":"ml-36","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which technique reduces unnecessary Dataverse 'modified' trigger runs so the flow only fires when specific columns change?","options":["Use filter (select) columns on the trigger","Add more approvers","Disable the connector","Use a longer timeout"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3A","topic":"Filtering Trigger Runs","sub":null,"explanation":"Triggers that run when a row is modified can use filtering (select) columns so the flow only runs when one of the defined columns is modified, preventing the flow from running unnecessarily.","source":{"title":"Create a cloud flow with Microsoft Dataverse — filter columns","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-automate/connection-cds"}},{"id":"ml-37","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"A flow uses 'Start and wait for an approval' with the 'Approve/Reject - First to respond' type. When do the actions after the approval step run?","options":["Only after every assigned approver responds","After any one approver approves or rejects","After a fixed 24-hour wait","Never — this type doesn't wait"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3A","topic":"Approval Types","sub":null,"explanation":"With 'Approve/Reject - First to respond', approval or rejection by any single approver completes the request, and the actions following the Start and wait for an approval action run after that first response. 'Everyone must approve' instead waits for all approvers.","source":{"title":"Get started with approvals — Approvals actions","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-automate/get-started-approvals"}},{"id":"ml-38","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which approval action lets a flow pause and wait for the approvers' response before continuing the run?","options":["Create an approval","Start and wait for an approval","Get response details","Post a message"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3A","topic":"Approval Action","sub":null,"explanation":"The 'Start and wait for an approval' action starts the approval and waits for the approvers' response before the flow continues. It's the quickest way to add an approval and capture the response in subsequent conditions.","source":{"title":"Get started with approvals","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-automate/get-started-approvals"}},{"id":"ml-39","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"After an approval action, how does a flow typically branch based on the outcome?","options":["By adding a Condition that checks whether the Approval response equals 'Approve' and putting actions in the If yes / If no branches","By deleting the trigger","By adding a rollup column","By changing the environment type"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3A","topic":"Condition After Approval","sub":null,"explanation":"A Condition action checks the approval Response (for example, is equal to 'Approve'); the If yes branch handles approved items and the If no branch handles rejected ones. Note approval response values are case sensitive.","source":{"title":"Create and test an approval workflow with Power Automate","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-automate/modern-approvals"}},{"id":"ml-40","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"A maker wants to validate a flow without waiting for a real event. What is the correct approach in the designer?","options":["Delete and re-create the flow","Select Test, choose Manually / 'I'll perform the trigger action', then fire the trigger to watch the run","Export the flow as a solution","Disable the connector"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3A","topic":"Testing a Flow","sub":null,"explanation":"In the designer you select Test, choose to run it manually ('I'll perform the trigger action'), then perform the trigger action; the flow runs in test mode so you can monitor each step. Run history then shows success/failure of each action.","source":{"title":"Create approval flows with attachments — Test your flow","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-automate/approval-attachments"}},{"id":"ml-41","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Where in Power Apps or Power Automate does a maker go to build a custom AI prompt?","options":["AI hub > Prompts > Build your own prompt","Settings > Users","Solutions > Import","Tables > New column"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3B","topic":"Build a Prompt","sub":null,"explanation":"In Power Apps or Power Automate, makers select AI hub > Prompts > Build your own prompt to open prompt builder, where they can start from a template or blank, define inputs, choose a model, and test the prompt before using it.","source":{"title":"Create a prompt","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/create-custom-prompt"}},{"id":"ml-42","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"What is the purpose of defining inputs on a custom prompt in prompt builder?","options":["To pass dynamic values to the prompt at runtime","To assign security roles","To set the environment region","To create a rollup column"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3B","topic":"Prompt Inputs","sub":null,"explanation":"Inputs (text, image, or document) let you incorporate dynamic runtime content into a prompt. You add an input by typing / or selecting Add content, then provide a sample value to test, so the prompt can receive different values each time it's run.","source":{"title":"Create a prompt — Configure and test your prompt","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/create-custom-prompt"}},{"id":"ml-43","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"After adding a custom prompt as a data source in a canvas app, how is it invoked in a formula?","options":["By calling its name with the .Predict function, passing required arguments","By adding it to a business process flow","By creating a rollup column","By importing it as a managed solution"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3B","topic":"Consume a Prompt in Power Apps","sub":null,"explanation":"A custom prompt is added from the Add data menu and used as a Power Fx function by calling .Predict on its name and passing arguments — for example Set(result, 'Task identifier'.Predict(TextInput1.Text)); result.text returns the generated text.","source":{"title":"Use your prompt in Power Apps","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/ai-builder/use-a-custom-prompt-in-app"}},{"id":"ml-44","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which Power Automate action runs an existing AI Builder prompt inside a cloud flow (renamed from 'Create text with GPT using a prompt' in May 2025)?","options":["Run a prompt","Start and wait for an approval","Compose","Get a row by ID"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3B","topic":"Consume a Prompt in a Flow","sub":null,"explanation":"The 'Run a prompt' action (formerly 'Create text with GPT using a prompt') adds a prompt to a flow so it can generate text inside an automated process. You select the prompt and fill its inputs with content from previous steps.","source":{"title":"Use your prompt in Power Automate","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/ai-builder/use-a-custom-prompt-in-flow"}},{"id":"ml-45","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"A prompt needs to answer using the organization's own business data instead of only the model's training data. Which technique do you use?","options":["Add knowledge (Retrieval Augmented Generation) by selecting a data source such as Dataverse columns","Increase the model temperature to 1","Add a rollup column","Convert the prompt to a business rule"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3B","topic":"Add Knowledge to a Prompt","sub":null,"explanation":"Adding knowledge implements Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG): you select a data source object (e.g., Dataverse columns) under the Knowledge section to augment the model with business-specific data, and you can filter the retrieved knowledge by attribute or input.","source":{"title":"Add knowledge to your prompt","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/use-your-own-prompt-data"}},{"id":"ml-46","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"AI Builder prebuilt prompts and models can be consumed in which of the following?","options":["Power Apps, Power Automate flows, and Dataverse low-code plug-ins","Only in Excel","Only in SharePoint pages","Only in the Exchange admin center"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3B","topic":"Prebuilt AI Models","sub":null,"explanation":"Prebuilt prompts and AI Builder models can be consumed in Power Apps (like custom prompts), in Power Automate flows, and in Dataverse low-code plug-ins (for example, using AISummarize to populate a column). AI Builder is integrated directly into Power Apps and Power Automate.","source":{"title":"Get started with prebuilt prompts","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/ai-builder/prebuilt-prompts"}},{"id":"ml-47","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"From where in Power Automate does a maker build AI Builder models to add intelligence to a flow?","options":["The AI models option in the left navigation pane","The Exchange admin center","The Dataverse view designer","Power BI Desktop"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3B","topic":"AI Builder in Power Automate","sub":null,"explanation":"In Power Automate, makers build AI Builder models using the AI models option in the left navigation pane, then use those models in flows to add intelligence such as prediction, extraction, or classification.","source":{"title":"Use AI Builder in Power Automate","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-automate/use-ai-builder"}},{"id":"ml-48","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"A business rule is defined for a Dataverse table (not a specific form). Where does its logic apply?","options":["Only in the classic web client","To all the table's forms and at the server level, in both canvas and model-driven apps that use the table","Only inside cloud flows","Only in Power BI"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3C","topic":"Business Rules Scope","sub":null,"explanation":"Business rules defined for a table apply to all the table's forms and at the server level, and they apply to both canvas apps and model-driven apps if the table is used in the app. Business rules let you apply logic and validations without writing code.","source":{"title":"Apply business logic in Microsoft Dataverse","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/data-platform/processes"}},{"id":"ml-49","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"What are the structural limits for a business process flow in Dataverse?","options":["Up to 30 stages, with up to 30 steps per stage","Exactly 1 stage and 1 step","Unlimited stages and steps","Up to 5 stages only"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3C","topic":"Business Process Flow Limits","sub":null,"explanation":"A business process flow can have up to 30 stages, and each stage can have up to 30 steps. There can be no more than 10 activated business process flows per table, and a multi-table process can span no more than five tables.","source":{"title":"Create or edit a business process flow","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/dynamics365/customerengagement/on-premises/customize/create-business-process-flow"}},{"id":"ml-50","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Before users can use a newly created business process flow, what must the maker do?","options":["Nothing — drafts are immediately usable","Activate it (and set security roles); while it remains a draft, people can't use it","Convert it to a canvas app","Add it to a Power BI dashboard"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3C","topic":"Activating a Business Process Flow","sub":null,"explanation":"As long as a business process flow is a draft, people can't use it. To make it available you must order it, enable security roles, and Activate it. You can also edit security roles to control who can create/read/update the process instance.","source":{"title":"Create or edit a business process flow","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/dynamics365/customerengagement/on-premises/customize/create-business-process-flow"}},{"id":"ml-51","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which aggregate functions can a Dataverse rollup column use over related rows?","options":["SUM, COUNT, MIN, MAX, AVG","CONCAT and SPLIT only","Only SUM","Custom JavaScript functions"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3C","topic":"Rollup Columns","sub":null,"explanation":"Rollup columns aggregate values over related rows using SUM, COUNT, MIN, MAX, and AVG. They support filters, integrate into forms/views/charts, and are recalculated by a recurring system job (default minimum recurrence one hour) or via the online Recalculate button.","source":{"title":"Define rollup columns that aggregate values","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/data-platform/define-rollup-fields"}},{"id":"ml-52","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"What best distinguishes a calculated column from a rollup column in Dataverse?","options":["A calculated column derives its value from a formula on the current row (computed when used); a rollup column aggregates values from related rows via a periodic job","They are identical","Only rollup columns can use a formula","Calculated columns aggregate related rows; rollup columns don't"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3C","topic":"Calculated vs Rollup Columns","sub":null,"explanation":"A calculated column assigns a value from a formula based on columns in the same (or related) row and is evaluated when used. A rollup column aggregates (SUM/COUNT/etc.) values across related rows and is computed by a recurring background job, not in real time.","source":{"title":"Create and edit columns in Dataverse — Calculated or Rollup","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/data-platform/create-edit-field-portal"}},{"id":"ml-53","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which statement about Dataverse rollup columns is correct?","options":["A rollup can aggregate over many-to-many (N:N) relationships","A rollup can only aggregate over one-to-many (1:N) related rows and can't reference another rollup column","A rollup updates instantly on every related-row change","A rollup can trigger a workflow when it updates"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3C","topic":"Rollup Restrictions","sub":null,"explanation":"A rollup can only be done over 1:N related rows (not N:N), and a rollup formula can't reference another rollup column or a complex calculated column. Rollups don't raise events, so they can't trigger workflows, and they update via a periodic job rather than instantly.","source":{"title":"Define rollup columns — considerations","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/data-platform/define-rollup-fields"}},{"id":"ml-54","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which trigger type starts a cloud flow automatically in response to an event such as a new SharePoint item or a new Dataverse row?","options":["An automated cloud flow trigger (e.g., 'When an item is created')","A manual button trigger only","A business rule","A rollup column"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3A","topic":"Cloud Flow Triggers","sub":null,"explanation":"An automated cloud flow uses an event-based trigger such as 'When an item is created' (SharePoint) or 'When a row is added, modified, or deleted' (Dataverse). The trigger defines when the flow runs; subsequent actions perform the work.","source":{"title":"Wait for approval in Power Automate — trigger the flow","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-automate/wait-for-approvals"}},{"id":"ml-55","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"In prompt builder, what does adjusting the model's temperature setting control?","options":["The randomness and creativity of the generated text","The number of approvers required","The Dataverse row-level security","How many tables the prompt can reference"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3B","topic":"Model & Temperature Settings","sub":null,"explanation":"When configuring a prompt you can select the chat model and settings such as temperature, which controls the randomness and creativity of the generated text. Lower temperature gives more deterministic output; higher temperature gives more varied output.","source":{"title":"Use prompts to make your agent perform tasks — prompt editor settings","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/nlu-prompt-node"}},{"id":"ml-56","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"What is a primary advantage of using business rules in Dataverse?","options":["They apply logic and validations without writing code or creating plug-ins","They replace the need for any security roles","They can only run inside Power BI","They require a pro developer to deploy"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3C","topic":"Business Rules vs Code","sub":null,"explanation":"Business rules and recommendations let you apply logic and validations without writing code or creating plug-ins, providing a simple interface to implement and maintain commonly used, fast-changing rules that apply consistently across the table.","source":{"title":"Apply business logic in Microsoft Dataverse","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/data-platform/processes"}},{"id":"ml-101","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"What is true of the Default environment in a Power Platform tenant?","options":["There is one per tenant, shared by all users, and new users are automatically added to its Environment Maker role","Each user gets their own private default environment","It can't contain a Dataverse database","It is automatically deleted after 30 days"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1A","topic":"Default Environment","sub":null,"explanation":"A single Default environment is automatically created per tenant and shared by all users. Whenever a user signs up for Power Apps they're automatically added to the Environment Maker role of the default environment.","source":{"title":"ALM basics with Microsoft Power Platform — Environments","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-platform/alm/basics-alm"}},{"id":"ml-102","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which statement about Power Platform solutions is correct?","options":["Solutions contain business data such as table rows","Solutions contain components (apps, flows, tables, columns, agents) and metadata but no business data","Solutions can only contain canvas apps","A solution can be any size with no limit"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1A","topic":"What a Solution Contains","sub":null,"explanation":"Solutions package components — model-driven and canvas apps, flows, tables, columns, charts, agents, and more — plus metadata, but they don't contain business data. They're exported/imported to move customizations between environments.","source":{"title":"ALM basics with Microsoft Power Platform — Solutions","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-platform/alm/basics-alm"}},{"id":"ml-103","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Using the Data workspace table designer, which action lets you add a new field to a table, specifying its name, data type, and format?","options":["New row","New column","Show/hide columns","Refresh"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1B","topic":"Data Workspace","sub":null,"explanation":"In the Data workspace table designer, New column creates a new data column for the table, where you specify the display name, data type, and format. New row adds data; Show/hide columns toggles visibility; Refresh reloads data.","source":{"title":"How to create and modify Dataverse tables by using the Data workspace","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-pages/configure/data-workspace-tables"}},{"id":"ml-104","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"How many prompt columns can be created per Dataverse table?","options":["Up to 5","Up to 50","Up to 200","Unlimited"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1B","topic":"Prompt Column Limits","sub":null,"explanation":"You can create up to five prompt columns per table. When creating one, you select the Prompt data type, then add or write the prompt that ties to input columns and generates the stored AI result.","source":{"title":"Prompt columns — Create a prompt column","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/data-platform/prompt-column"}},{"id":"ml-105","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"In a single business unit (no hierarchy), which access level grants a user access to a record only when they own it, belong to the owning team, or it was shared with them?","options":["Global","Deep","Basic","None"],"correct":[2],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1B","topic":"Access Levels","sub":null,"explanation":"Basic access provides access to records the user owns, records owned by a team they belong to, or records shared with them. Local gives access to all data in the business unit; Global/Deep apply only with a business-unit hierarchy; None blocks access.","source":{"title":"Security concepts for developers","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/developer/data-platform/security-concepts"}},{"id":"ml-106","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"To make a Copilot row summary respect each user's preferred language, what should the maker include in the summary prompt?","options":["Nothing — it always uses the user's language","The LanguageCode input plus an instruction like 'You must respond in language <LanguageCode>'","A rollup column","A separate business rule per language"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1B","topic":"Row Summary Language","sub":null,"explanation":"By default the summary is generated in the language of the prompt itself. To respect the user's language, include the LanguageCode input and add the instruction 'You must respond in language <LanguageCode>'. If Copilot doesn't support the user's language, English is used.","source":{"title":"Configure a row summary for a model-driven app — Write a good prompt","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/data-platform/configure-form-row-summary"}},{"id":"ml-107","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which designer is used to create and edit dashboards that present visualizations such as embedded Power BI reports, charts, and views?","options":["Form designer","View designer","Dashboard designer","Chart designer"],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2A","topic":"Model-Driven Designers","sub":null,"explanation":"The Dashboard designer (accessed via the table designer) creates and edits dashboards that present different visualizations — embedded Power BI reports, data charts, and views. The Form, View, and Chart designers build those individual components.","source":{"title":"Meet the model-driven app designers","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/model-driven-apps/model-driven-designers"}},{"id":"ml-108","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which security role does a maker need to build canvas apps on Dataverse in an environment?","options":["System Administrator only","The Environment Maker role (directly or via a Dataverse team mapped to an Entra security group)","Global Admin in Microsoft 365","No role is required"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2B","topic":"Maker Security Role","sub":null,"explanation":"A canvas app maker needs the Environment Maker security role, granted either directly or through a Dataverse team that uses the Microsoft Entra ID security group category. Custom security roles aren't currently supported for canvas app maker scenarios.","source":{"title":"Create a canvas app using Microsoft Dataverse — Prerequisites","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/canvas-apps/data-platform-create-app-scratch"}},{"id":"ml-109","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"What happens to the link between a consuming app and a component library if the maker edits the component directly inside the app instead of in the library?","options":["Nothing changes — updates still flow automatically","The app becomes self-contained for that component and no longer receives library updates","The component is deleted from the app","The library is converted to a managed solution"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2B","topic":"Component Library ALM","sub":null,"explanation":"When a component is imported into an app its definition is copied in, and editing it locally makes the app self-contained for that component with no direct link to the library — so no further update prompts are received. To keep the relationship, make changes in the component library instead.","source":{"title":"Component library application lifecycle management (ALM)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/canvas-apps/component-library-alm"}},{"id":"ml-110","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"In which canvas app properties can the Trace function be called to write structured records to Live monitor?","options":["Behavior properties such as OnSelect, OnChange, OnVisible, and OnStart","Data properties such as a label's Text","Only in the App.Formulas property","Only inside a cloud flow"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2B","topic":"Monitor + Trace","sub":null,"explanation":"Trace works only in behavior properties (OnSelect, OnChange, OnVisible, OnStart). Because you can't place Trace in data properties like a label's Text, makers use temporary debug buttons to capture those values for Monitor.","source":{"title":"Debug Canvas apps by using Live monitor and Trace","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/power-platform/power-apps/create-and-use-apps/monitor-debugging-canvas-apps"}},{"id":"ml-111","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Before adding a custom Copilot Studio agent to a canvas app, the agent must be published in the same environment and configured with what?","options":["A rollup column","User authentication in Microsoft Entra ID, with makers/users given access","A business process flow","A managed solution export"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2B","topic":"Add Custom Copilot to Canvas App","sub":null,"explanation":"To add a custom Copilot to a canvas app, the agent must be created and published in Copilot Studio in the same environment, configured with user authentication in Microsoft Entra ID, and shared so makers and users have access. You then enable App Copilot in the app settings and connect it.","source":{"title":"Add a custom Copilot to a canvas app","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/canvas-apps/add-custom-copilot"}},{"id":"ml-112","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which approval type requests approvals one at a time in a specific order, requiring each approver to respond before moving to the next?","options":["Approve/Reject - First to respond","Sequential approval","Custom Responses - Wait for one response","Approve/Reject - Everyone must approve"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3A","topic":"Sequential Approval","sub":null,"explanation":"Sequential approval requests approvals one at a time in a defined order; each approver must respond before the request moves to the next approver, and the following actions run after all approvers in the sequence respond.","source":{"title":"Get started with approvals — Approvals actions","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-automate/get-started-approvals"}},{"id":"ml-113","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"In a cloud flow, what do the two branches of a Condition action represent?","options":["Two different connectors","Actions to run if the condition is met (True / If yes) and if it isn't met (False / If no)","The start and end of the flow","Two separate environments"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3A","topic":"Condition Branches","sub":null,"explanation":"A Condition action has two branches: the True (If yes) branch runs when the condition is met, and the False (If no) branch runs when it isn't. For example, on an approved file the True branch creates and the False branch does nothing further.","source":{"title":"Trigger approvals from a SharePoint document library — Configure your flow","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-automate/trigger-sharepoint-library"}},{"id":"ml-114","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"In Power Apps and Power Automate, which credits do AI Builder actions consume first?","options":["Copilot Credits only","AI Builder credits first, then Copilot Credits if exhausted","Azure subscription credits","No credits are consumed"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3B","topic":"AI Builder Credits","sub":null,"explanation":"In Power Apps or Power Automate, AI Builder actions consume AI Builder credits first; if those are exhausted or unavailable, they consume Copilot Credits instead. In Copilot Studio (agents/agent flows), AI Builder actions consume Copilot Credits only.","source":{"title":"All AI Builder credits have been consumed — Cause","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/power-platform/ai-builder/power-automate/all-ai-builder-credits-have-been-consumed"}},{"id":"ml-115","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"When a business process flow spans two tables, why should a maker set a Relationship between consecutive stages?","options":["It encrypts the data","Relationships carry over mapped column data between rows (minimizing data entry) and list related rows when moving to the next stage","It activates the flow automatically","It is required for rollup columns"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3C","topic":"BPF Stage Relationship","sub":null,"explanation":"Setting a relationship between stages based on different tables lets defined column maps automatically carry data between rows, minimizing data entry, and surfaces related rows when the user selects Next stage, promoting reuse of records in the process.","source":{"title":"Tutorial: Create a business process flow — stages","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-automate/create-business-process-flow"}},{"id":"ml-116","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"A formula column in Dataverse is authored with which language?","options":["Power Fx","DAX","JavaScript","M (Power Query)"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3C","topic":"Formula Columns","sub":null,"explanation":"Dataverse formula columns are authored with Power Fx (the same low-code expression language used in canvas apps). They complement calculated and rollup columns for building business logic that travels with the data regardless of which app accesses it.","source":{"title":"Power Fx formula reference overview — Dataverse formula columns","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-platform/power-fx/formula-reference-overview"}},{"id":"gen-1","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A department lead describes a business process in plain English and wants Power Apps to produce a working app together with the underlying tables, without anyone writing code. Which AI-enabled capability best fits this requirement?","options":["Copilot in Power Apps","A managed solution import","A Dataverse rollup column","The classic solution explorer"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Copilot in Power Apps lets makers describe a need in natural language and generates both an app and its data model with no coding. Solution imports, rollup columns, and the solution explorer do not create apps from a description.","source":{"title":"Build apps with Copilot in Power Apps — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/canvas-apps/ai-overview"}},{"id":"gen-2","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"You are designing a solution deployment strategy. Development happens in one environment and the app must be promoted to test and then production without letting downstream users edit the components directly. Which solution type should you deploy to the test and production environments?","options":["An unmanaged solution in every environment","A managed solution","The Default solution","A solution with no publisher"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"You author in unmanaged solutions in development and deploy managed solutions to downstream environments; managed components are locked so they cannot be edited directly in test or production. This is the core of healthy Power Platform ALM.","source":{"title":"Solution concepts — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-platform/alm/solution-concepts-alm"}},{"id":"gen-3","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An administrator wants app users to ask natural-language questions about their model-driven app data through Microsoft 365 Copilot. Before any user can see the Copilot experience in the app, what must happen?","options":["The app must be converted to a canvas app","Microsoft 365 Copilot must be enabled for the environment and app","A business process flow must be added to every table","The app must be exported as an unmanaged solution"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Microsoft 365 Copilot for model-driven apps must be enabled at the environment and/or app level before it becomes visible, and users additionally need both a Power Apps premium license and a Microsoft 365 Copilot license.","source":{"title":"Add Microsoft 365 Copilot in model-driven apps — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/model-driven-apps/add-microsoft-365-copilot"}},{"id":"gen-4","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker needs a single-user space to build and test Power Apps, flows, and Dataverse tables for learning purposes, but the environment must not be used to run or share production apps. Which environment type should be provisioned?","options":["Production","Default","Sandbox","Developer"],"correct":[3],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A Developer environment is a single-user space intended for building, testing, and learning that cannot run or share production apps. Production is for live apps, Default is shared by all users, and Sandbox is a multi-user non-production environment.","source":{"title":"Application lifecycle management (ALM) basics — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-platform/alm/basics-alm"}},{"id":"gen-5","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"Your team is planning environments for a new intelligent application. You need a non-production environment that supports copy, reset, and restore operations so testing can start from a known state repeatedly. Which environment type meets this need?","options":["Default","Developer","Sandbox","Trial"],"correct":[2],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Sandbox environments are non-production environments that support administrative operations such as copy, reset, and restore, making them ideal for isolated development and testing. Default and Developer do not offer the same reset/copy tooling, and Trial environments expire.","source":{"title":"Application lifecycle management (ALM) basics — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-platform/alm/basics-alm"}},{"id":"gen-6","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"During solution design you must ensure that every custom table and column your team creates carries a consistent naming prefix so components are clearly attributed to your organization. Which solution element defines that customization prefix?","options":["The solution publisher","The environment region","The Dataverse security role","The app's site map"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The publisher associated with a solution defines the customization prefix and the option value prefix applied to components created in it. Choosing a meaningful publisher up front keeps naming consistent across the solution.","source":{"title":"Solution concepts — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-platform/alm/solution-concepts-alm"}},{"id":"gen-7","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A governance review flags that a business-critical app is running in the tenant's Default environment. Why is the Default environment generally a poor choice for hosting an enterprise production app?","options":["It cannot store Dataverse tables at all","It blocks the use of any Copilot features","It is automatically created and shared by all users in the tenant, making it hard to govern","It only permits managed solutions to be imported"],"correct":[2],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The Default environment is created automatically and is shared by every user in the tenant, so it is difficult to secure and govern for a critical app. Microsoft recommends creating dedicated environments for important workloads.","source":{"title":"Application lifecycle management (ALM) basics — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-platform/alm/basics-alm"}},{"id":"gen-8","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A large application is being split so that reference data, core tables, and a customer-facing app can each be developed and shipped on their own cadence by different teams. Which solution-organization approach supports this requirement?","options":["Put every component in one large unmanaged solution","Store all components only in the Default solution","Divide the components across multiple solutions, each owning a related set of components","Avoid solutions and edit components directly in production"],"correct":[2],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Organizing work into multiple focused solutions lets independent teams build and deploy related components on separate schedules while managing dependencies deliberately. A single monolithic solution or the Default solution makes independent shipping and dependency control harder.","source":{"title":"Organize your solutions — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-platform/alm/organize-solutions"}},{"id":"gen-9","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"Requirements call for a conversational agent that answers customer questions using your own knowledge sources and custom topics, going beyond the ready-made Copilot experiences. Which tool should you choose to build this agent?","options":["A Dataverse business rule","Microsoft Copilot Studio","A canvas app gallery control","A rollup column"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Microsoft Copilot Studio is the low-code tool for authoring custom agents with your own topics and knowledge when built-in Copilot experiences are not enough. Business rules, gallery controls, and rollup columns do not create conversational agents.","source":{"title":"Application lifecycle management (ALM) basics — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-platform/alm/basics-alm"}},{"id":"gen-10","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"You export a solution as managed and import it into production. Later a maker tries to delete one of its columns directly in production and cannot. Which principle of healthy ALM does this behavior enforce?","options":["Managed components are locked in the target so changes flow from the development environment","Managed solutions convert automatically to unmanaged on import","Production environments disable all customization for every solution","Only the Default solution can be edited anywhere"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"In healthy ALM, changes are made in the development environment and flow downstream as managed solutions, so managed components are locked against direct edits in the target. This preserves a single source of truth for customizations.","source":{"title":"Solution concepts — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-platform/alm/solution-concepts-alm"}},{"id":"gen-11","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A solution architect wants to deploy only a subset of an existing solution's components to production, avoiding unintended dependencies from unrelated components. Which capability should be used?","options":["Importing the solution as unmanaged","Adding all components to the Default solution","Turning off the solution publisher","Solution segmentation to export only the selected components"],"correct":[3],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Solution segmentation lets you export only the specific components and subcomponents you need, which helps avoid shipping unnecessary dependencies. Importing everything or using the Default solution does not give that fine-grained control.","source":{"title":"Organize your solutions — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-platform/alm/organize-solutions"}},{"id":"gen-12","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A team wants to try Power Platform capabilities for a short evaluation period, understanding the environment will be removed automatically after it lapses. Which environment type matches this short-lived evaluation need?","options":["Trial","Production","Sandbox","Developer"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A Trial environment is meant for short-term evaluation and expires after its trial period, after which it is removed. Production, Sandbox, and Developer environments persist and are used for ongoing work.","source":{"title":"Application lifecycle management (ALM) basics — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-platform/alm/basics-alm"}},{"id":"gen-13","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"When evaluating built-in versus custom AI options for a project, at what point does extending with a custom prompt or agent become the right design choice?","options":["Whenever any Copilot feature exists in the product","Only when the app is a model-driven app","When the ready-made built-in Copilot experiences do not fully satisfy the specific requirement","Only when the solution is unmanaged"],"correct":[2],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"You evaluate the built-in Copilot and agent experiences first, and turn to extensibility options such as custom prompts or Copilot Studio agents when the built-in capabilities do not meet the specific requirement. App type and solution management state are not the deciding factors.","source":{"title":"Build apps with Copilot in Power Apps — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/canvas-apps/ai-overview"}},{"id":"gen-14","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"During design, which statement best describes what a Power Platform solution is used for?","options":["It is a security role that grants table permissions","It is a container that packages components so they can be transported across environments","It is a report that visualizes Dataverse data","It is a connector that authenticates to external services"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A solution is a container that packages apps, tables, flows, and other components so they can be moved between environments as part of ALM. Security roles, reports, and connectors serve different purposes.","source":{"title":"Solution concepts — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-platform/alm/solution-concepts-alm"}},{"id":"gen-15","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker analyzing requirements wants the fastest AI-assisted path to stand up a data-backed app from an idea, then refine tables and screens iteratively. Which approach reflects using AI-enabled tooling to design the solution?","options":["Describe the app in natural language with Copilot in Power Apps to generate the app and tables, then refine","Hand-build every table in the classic solution explorer before any app exists","Write a plug-in in code to create the schema","Import a managed solution from an unrelated project and edit it in production"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Copilot in Power Apps generates an app and its data model from a natural-language description, which the maker can then iterate on, embodying AI-enabled solution design. The other options are slower, code-heavy, or violate ALM practices.","source":{"title":"Build apps with Copilot in Power Apps — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/canvas-apps/ai-overview"}},{"id":"gen-16","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"Your ALM strategy needs a reliable, repeatable way to move customizations from development to test and production while keeping a controlled history of changes. Which combination best supports this?","options":["Editing components directly in each environment by hand","Keeping everything in the Default solution and copying screenshots","Emailing exported files between makers with no versioning","Separate dev/test/production environments with solutions promoted through a source-controlled pipeline"],"correct":[3],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Healthy ALM uses separate environments for development, test, and production, with solutions exported and promoted through a source-controlled, automatable pipeline. Manual per-environment edits and unversioned file sharing undermine reliability and traceability.","source":{"title":"Application lifecycle management (ALM) basics — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-platform/alm/basics-alm"}},{"id":"gen-17","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"While organizing multiple solutions, one solution reuses a table defined in another. What must the design account for so the dependent solution can be deployed successfully?","options":["The dependent solution must be unmanaged in production","Every solution must share one publisher prefix","The dependency on the required component must be present in the target before or with the deployment","Solutions can never reference components from other solutions"],"correct":[2],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"When one solution depends on components owned by another, those required components must already exist in (or be deployed alongside) the target environment, or the import will fail on missing dependencies. Solutions can reference shared components as long as dependencies are satisfied.","source":{"title":"Organize your solutions — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-platform/alm/organize-solutions"}},{"id":"gen-18","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker asks why they should develop customizations inside an unmanaged solution rather than the Default solution. Which reason is correct?","options":["The Default solution cannot contain any tables","An unmanaged solution can be exported and packaged as managed for deployment, whereas the Default solution is not intended for transport","Components in the Default solution are automatically encrypted","Unmanaged solutions run only in Trial environments"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Working in a dedicated unmanaged solution lets you export it and generate a managed version to deploy downstream, supporting proper ALM; the Default solution is not designed to be transported between environments. The other statements are inaccurate.","source":{"title":"Solution concepts — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-platform/alm/solution-concepts-alm"}},{"id":"gen-19","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker opens the Power Apps data workspace and wants to build a new Dataverse table by describing it in plain language so that Copilot proposes the columns and adds sample rows. Which approach should they choose?","options":["Import the data from an Excel file first, then rename the generated columns","Start with a blank table and add every column manually before entering data","Use the Copilot-assisted 'describe the new table' experience in the data workspace","Create a virtual table connected to an external SQL Server source"],"correct":[2],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The data workspace lets a maker describe a table in natural language; Copilot then generates the schema and sample data, which is faster than blank creation or importing.","source":{"title":"Work with any data — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/maker/data-platform/work-with-any-data"}},{"id":"gen-20","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"While designing a data model, a maker needs to store customer contact details. Microsoft recommends reusing an existing table before creating a new one. The out-of-the-box Contact table is an example of which kind of table?","options":["A standard table that ships with Dataverse","A custom table the maker must build from scratch","A virtual table backed by an external system","An elastic table intended for high-volume telemetry"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Contact and Account are standard (out-of-the-box) tables provided with Dataverse; makers should reuse standard tables before creating custom ones.","source":{"title":"Work with any data — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/maker/data-platform/work-with-any-data"}},{"id":"gen-21","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker is creating a Dataverse table whose rows must be assignable to individual users and secured with record-level permissions per user or team. Which ownership type must be selected when the table is created?","options":["Organization-owned","None, because tables have no ownership concept","Team-only owned","User or team owned"],"correct":[3],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"User or team ownership enables record-level security so rows can be owned by and shared with specific users or teams; organization-owned rows can't be assigned to individuals.","source":{"title":"Work with any data — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/maker/data-platform/work-with-any-data"}},{"id":"gen-22","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"When a new Dataverse table is created, one text column is generated automatically and is used as the default label shown in lookups and search results. What is this column called?","options":["The primary key column","The primary name (primary) column","The row-version GUID column","The status reason column"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Every table has a primary name column (a single line of text) used as the display value in lookups and links; the primary key is a separate GUID column.","source":{"title":"Create and edit columns — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/maker/data-platform/create-edit-field-portal"}},{"id":"gen-23","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker must add a column that lets users pick a single value from a fixed, reusable list of options that will be shared and centrally maintained across several tables. Which column configuration best fits?","options":["A Yes/No (Boolean) column","A single line of text column with an input mask","A Choice column bound to a global choice (option set)","A whole number column with a defined range"],"correct":[2],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A Choice column backed by a global choice provides a reusable, centrally managed option set that multiple tables and columns can reference, unlike a local choice or free text.","source":{"title":"Create and edit columns — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/maker/data-platform/create-edit-field-portal"}},{"id":"gen-24","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"You need each Order row to reference exactly one Customer, while a single Customer can have many Orders. What should you add to the Order table?","options":["A lookup column, which creates a many-to-one relationship to Customer","A many-to-many relationship between Order and Customer","A rollup column that summarizes the Customer table","A choice column that lists all customers"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A lookup column on the 'many' side (Order) establishes the many-to-one / one-to-many relationship to Customer, so each order points to a single customer.","source":{"title":"Create and edit one-to-many relationships — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/maker/data-platform/create-edit-1n-relationships"}},{"id":"gen-25","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A Student can enroll in many Courses and each Course has many Students, and the business does not need to store any attributes about the enrollment itself. Which relationship should you configure?","options":["Two separate one-to-many lookup relationships","A many-to-many relationship, which Dataverse implements with a system intersect table","A hierarchical self-referential relationship on Student","A calculated column added to each table"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A many-to-many relationship links both tables through a system-managed intersect table; you'd only model a manual intersect table with two lookups when you must store data about the association.","source":{"title":"Create and edit many-to-many relationships — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/maker/data-platform/create-edit-nn-relationships-solution-explorer"}},{"id":"gen-26","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"In a one-to-many relationship from Account (parent) to Contact (child), the business wants deleting an Account to also delete its related Contacts. Which relationship setting controls this?","options":["The relationship's column (field) mapping","The lookup column's searchable setting","Record-level security sharing on each Contact","The relationship behavior (cascade) set to Parental / Cascade All on Delete"],"correct":[3],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Relationship (cascade) behaviors such as Parental determine what happens to related rows on actions like Delete, Assign, and Share; Parental cascades the delete to child Contacts.","source":{"title":"Create and edit one-to-many relationships — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/maker/data-platform/create-edit-1n-relationships"}},{"id":"gen-27","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker adds a column to a Dataverse table whose value is produced by a natural-language AI prompt that can reference other columns in the same row, for example to summarize a long description. Which column type is this?","options":["A rollup column","A calculated column","A prompt column","A formula column"],"correct":[2],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A prompt column embeds a generative AI (AI Builder) prompt in the table and generates its value from the row's data; rollup, calculated, and formula columns use deterministic expressions, not AI.","source":{"title":"Prompt columns — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/maker/data-platform/prompt-column"}},{"id":"gen-28","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"Which statement about prompt columns in Dataverse is correct?","options":["The column's value is produced by a generative AI prompt and can draw on other columns in the same row","Prompt columns store only manually typed text and never call an AI model","Prompt columns require a Power Fx expression instead of a natural-language prompt","Prompt columns can be added only to virtual tables"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A prompt column runs a generative AI prompt that uses the row's data to produce output, such as a summary or classification, rather than storing manually entered values.","source":{"title":"Prompt columns — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/maker/data-platform/prompt-column"}},{"id":"gen-29","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"On a record's main form, a maker wants Copilot to display a concise natural-language summary of the record's key fields at the top of the form. Which feature should they configure?","options":["A business rule that concatenates the fields into one text column","A rollup column that aggregates related rows","A calculated column that formats the fields","The row summary (Copilot record summary) feature on the form"],"correct":[3],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Row summaries let Copilot generate an AI summary of a record shown on the form; the maker chooses which columns feed the summary. Rollup, calculated columns, and business rules can't produce a natural-language overview.","source":{"title":"Configure record (row) summary on a form — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/maker/data-platform/configure-form-row-summary"}},{"id":"gen-30","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker creates a system view that should be available to every user of a model-driven app and selectable in the app's view dropdown. Which kind of view must they create?","options":["A personal view shared with the maker's team","A public view","An associated view on a related table","A quick find view only"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Public views are system views available to all users and appear in the app's view selector; personal views are private to their owner unless explicitly shared.","source":{"title":"Data workspace views — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-pages/configure/data-workspace-views"}},{"id":"gen-31","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"In the view designer, a maker wants a public view to show only active rows, sorted by the date each row was created. What do they configure on the view?","options":["Filter criteria for Status equals Active and a sort on Created On","A cascade behavior on the table's relationship","A rollup aggregation across related rows","Column-level security on the Created On column"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A view is defined by its column layout, filter criteria, and sort order; here you filter on Status = Active and sort by Created On. Cascade, rollup, and column security don't shape a view's contents.","source":{"title":"Data workspace views — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-pages/configure/data-workspace-views"}},{"id":"gen-32","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"Which form type is the primary interface for creating and editing a single record in a model-driven app, supporting tabs, sections, subgrids, and business rules?","options":["Quick create form","Quick view form","Main form","Card form"],"correct":[2],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The main form is the full editing surface for a record, with tabs, sections, and subgrids; quick create forms capture a few fields fast and quick view forms show read-only related data.","source":{"title":"Work with any data — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/maker/data-platform/work-with-any-data"}},{"id":"gen-33","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An administrator must grant a group of users the ability to read Contact records only within their own business unit, not across the whole organization. What is the primary mechanism?","options":["Column-level (field) security profiles","Sharing each Contact record individually","A Dataverse team that owns every Contact","A security role with the Read privilege set to the Business Unit access level"],"correct":[3],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Security roles define privileges and access levels (User, Business Unit, Parent:Child Business Units, Organization); setting Read to Business Unit scopes access to the user's own business unit.","source":{"title":"Security concepts in Dataverse — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/developer/data-platform/security-concepts"}},{"id":"gen-34","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A table has a Salary column that only HR staff may view or edit, even though all users can open the record. Which Dataverse feature enforces this at the data layer?","options":["A table-level privilege in the users' security role","Column (field-level) security using a field security profile","A business rule that hides the column on the form","A public view that omits the column"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Column-level security restricts read, update, and create on a specific column via field security profiles, independent of table-level role privileges. A business rule only hides a field in the UI and is not a security boundary.","source":{"title":"Security concepts in Dataverse — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/developer/data-platform/security-concepts"}},{"id":"gen-35","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"In the Dataverse security model, which container groups users and teams to mirror an organizational structure and forms the root of the access-level hierarchy?","options":["Business unit","Environment","Solution","Application user"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Business units group users and teams and underpin access levels; every environment has a root business unit from which the hierarchy extends.","source":{"title":"Security in Microsoft Dataverse — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-platform/admin/wp-security-cds"}},{"id":"gen-36","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker created a table as Organization-owned but now needs individual users to own rows so records can be shared and assigned per user. What must they do?","options":["Toggle the ownership type in the existing table's settings","Add a lookup to the User table and rely on it for record security","Re-create the table with User or team ownership, because ownership can't be changed after creation","Convert the table into a virtual table"],"correct":[2],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A table's ownership type is set at creation and is immutable; to move from organization-owned to user or team ownership you must create a new table with the correct ownership.","source":{"title":"Work with any data — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/maker/data-platform/work-with-any-data"}},{"id":"gen-37","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A company needs a data-centric internal application where users navigate a structured set of related Dataverse tables through consistent forms, views, and dashboards, and they want the UI to adapt across devices with minimal manual design effort. Which app type best fits this requirement?","options":["A model-driven app","A canvas app built from a blank screen","A Power Pages public website","A SharePoint list form"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Model-driven apps are data-first and component-focused: you build them over Dataverse tables, and their forms and views render a responsive layout automatically, making them ideal for structured, data-centric scenarios.","source":{"title":"What are model-driven apps? — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/maker/model-driven-apps/model-driven-app-overview"}},{"id":"gen-38","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"In the model-driven app designer, which component defines the app's navigation structure of areas, groups, and subareas?","options":["The main form designer","An interactive dashboard","The site map (navigation)","A business process flow"],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The site map defines a model-driven app's navigation, organizing content into areas, groups, and subareas that point to tables, dashboards, or custom pages.","source":{"title":"Add or edit model-driven app components — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/maker/model-driven-apps/add-edit-app-components"}},{"id":"gen-39","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker wants users to add a new related contact quickly from a subgrid without leaving the current record, using a small streamlined data-entry form. Which form type should be configured?","options":["Main form","Quick create form","Quick view form","Card form"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A quick create form provides a compact, streamlined form for creating a new row inline (for example, from a subgrid or the + New command) without opening the full main form.","source":{"title":"Model-driven app designers — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/maker/model-driven-apps/model-driven-designers"}},{"id":"gen-40","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker needs to display several read-only fields from a related parent Account directly on the Contact main form so the user does not have to navigate to the account record. Which component should be added to the form?","options":["A subgrid of the account","A business rule","An editable grid","A quick view control (quick view form)"],"correct":[3],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A quick view control embeds a quick view form that shows read-only fields from a related (lookup) record directly on the current form, so users see the related data without navigating away.","source":{"title":"Model-driven app designers — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/maker/model-driven-apps/model-driven-designers"}},{"id":"gen-41","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker wants to build a page in a model-driven app by describing the desired layout and data in natural language and letting Copilot generate it. Which capability enables this?","options":["Generative pages","Embedding an external canvas app","Hand-coding a custom page in Power Fx","Configuring an interactive dashboard"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Generative pages let a maker describe an app page in natural language and have Copilot generate a working page within the model-driven app, which can then be refined.","source":{"title":"Generative pages in model-driven apps — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/maker/model-driven-apps/generative-pages"}},{"id":"gen-42","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"Which statement about a system chart in a model-driven app is correct?","options":["It is owned by an individual user and cannot be seen by anyone else","It can only visualize data from several unrelated tables at once","It is associated with a specific table and can be displayed alongside that table's views","It cannot honor the filters applied by the view it is shown with"],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A system chart is defined on a specific table and is available to users; it can be shown next to that table's grid/view and reflects the records the view returns.","source":{"title":"Customize visualizations and dashboards — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/developer/model-driven-apps/customize-visualizations-dashboards"}},{"id":"gen-43","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker wants a single landing page that combines charts, lists, and web resources so users get an at-a-glance operational overview when they open the app. Which component should they build?","options":["A quick view form","A dashboard","A business process flow","A personal view"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A dashboard aggregates multiple components such as charts, lists (views), and web resources onto one page to give users a consolidated overview.","source":{"title":"Customize visualizations and dashboards — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/developer/model-driven-apps/customize-visualizations-dashboards"}},{"id":"gen-44","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"How is a user's ability to open and use a specific model-driven app controlled?","options":["By manually sharing the app record with each user one at a time only","By adding the user's name into the app's site map","By publishing the app to a SharePoint site collection","By assigning the security role(s) associated with the app to the user"],"correct":[3],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Access to a model-driven app is granted through security roles: the roles associated with the app must be assigned to users (or their teams) for them to see and run it.","source":{"title":"What are model-driven apps? — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/maker/model-driven-apps/model-driven-app-overview"}},{"id":"gen-45","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A company needs sales users and service users to see different main forms for the Account table based on their job role. What should the maker configure?","options":["A separate canvas app for each role","One form containing all fields, hiding fields with JavaScript at runtime","Multiple main forms, each with the appropriate security roles assigned and ordered","A separate quick create form for each role"],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Model-driven apps support multiple main forms with security roles assigned per form (role-based forms); the ordered, role-assigned forms determine which form each user sees.","source":{"title":"Model-driven app designers — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/maker/model-driven-apps/model-driven-designers"}},{"id":"gen-46","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"When a maker adds a table to a model-driven app in the app designer, what becomes available to include for that table?","options":["The table's forms, views, and charts","Only the table's associated Power Automate flows","A brand-new Dataverse environment","A canvas app component library"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Adding a table to a model-driven app surfaces that table's assets — its forms, views, and charts — which the maker can then choose to include in the app.","source":{"title":"Add or edit model-driven app components — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/maker/model-driven-apps/add-edit-app-components"}},{"id":"gen-47","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"What distinguishes a public view from a personal view in a model-driven app?","options":["A personal view is automatically available to every user in the organization","A public view is created by makers/admins and available broadly, while a personal view is created by an end user for their own use and can be shared","A public view can never be set as the default view for a table","A personal view must be deployed through a solution using ALM"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Public (system) views are created by makers or admins and available to app users, whereas personal views are created by an individual user (Save Filters As) for their own use and may be shared with others.","source":{"title":"Add or edit model-driven app components — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/maker/model-driven-apps/add-edit-app-components"}},{"id":"gen-48","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker wants users to edit multiple rows inline directly within a grid, on a form or a view, without opening each record individually. Which control provides this?","options":["A read-only grid","A quick view form","A system chart","An editable grid"],"correct":[3],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The editable grid control lets users edit data inline across multiple rows and columns within the grid, without opening each row's form.","source":{"title":"Model-driven app designers — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/maker/model-driven-apps/model-driven-designers"}},{"id":"gen-49","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"Which designer is used to compose a model-driven app by selecting the tables, forms, views, and navigation the app will include?","options":["The app designer (modern model-driven app designer)","The Power Fx formula bar","The canvas screen designer","The AI Builder studio"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The model-driven app designer is where a maker assembles the app — adding pages/tables, choosing forms and views, and configuring the navigation (site map).","source":{"title":"What are model-driven apps? — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/maker/model-driven-apps/model-driven-app-overview"}},{"id":"gen-50","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"Why do makers not need to manually design a pixel-level layout for each device when building model-driven app forms?","options":["Because model-driven forms only run in a desktop browser","Because the maker must write custom CSS for each screen size","Because model-driven forms are metadata-driven and render a responsive layout automatically","Because each form is generated as an independent canvas app"],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Model-driven apps are metadata-driven: the platform generates a responsive UI from the form/component definitions, so the layout adapts across devices without manual pixel design.","source":{"title":"What are model-driven apps? — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/maker/model-driven-apps/model-driven-app-overview"}},{"id":"gen-51","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker wants a view that shows only active opportunities owned by the signed-in user, sorted by estimated close date. What should they configure on the view?","options":["A business rule attached to the opportunity form","The view's filter criteria (including an 'Owner equals current user' condition) and its sort order","A dashboard chart component","A quick create form for opportunities"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A view is defined by its columns, filter criteria, and sort order; adding an 'Owner equals current user' filter plus a status filter and a sort on estimated close date produces the required list.","source":{"title":"Add or edit model-driven app components — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/maker/model-driven-apps/add-edit-app-components"}},{"id":"gen-52","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"After building a model-driven app, what must the maker do so a group of business users can actually open and use it?","options":["Export the app as a canvas package and email it to each user","Add each user's name to the app's site map areas","Publish the app only to a Microsoft Teams channel","Publish/share the app and ensure the users have an appropriate security role"],"correct":[3],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"To make a model-driven app usable, the maker publishes and shares it, and the target users must have a security role that grants access to the app and its underlying data.","source":{"title":"What are model-driven apps? — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/maker/model-driven-apps/model-driven-app-overview"}},{"id":"gen-53","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An interactive dashboard in a model-driven app is primarily intended to help which kind of user?","options":["Service or case workers who need to triage and act on a workload using visual filters and streams","Only system administrators managing environment capacity","Makers building reusable canvas components","Anonymous external visitors browsing a public portal"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Interactive dashboards combine visual filters (charts) with streams of records so frontline workers, such as service agents, can triage and act on their workload from a single screen.","source":{"title":"Customize visualizations and dashboards — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/developer/model-driven-apps/customize-visualizations-dashboards"}},{"id":"gen-54","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A stakeholder asks whether to build a model-driven or a canvas app for a highly data-driven internal application spanning many related Dataverse tables with standardized data entry. Which choice and rationale is correct?","options":["Canvas, because it automatically provides metadata-driven forms over Dataverse","Canvas, because model-driven apps cannot connect to Dataverse tables","Model-driven, because it is data-first and generates consistent forms, views, and navigation over related Dataverse tables","Model-driven, because it forces the maker to hand-design a pixel-perfect UI for every screen"],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Model-driven apps are the better fit for structured, data-heavy scenarios: they are built on Dataverse and generate consistent forms, views, and navigation automatically, unlike canvas apps which are UI-first and manually laid out.","source":{"title":"What are model-driven apps? — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/maker/model-driven-apps/model-driven-app-overview"}},{"id":"gen-55","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A canvas app gallery is bound to a Dataverse table containing 50,000 rows, and the maker sees a blue delegation warning on the Items property. Which change ensures every matching row is returned rather than only the first page?","options":["Increase the gallery's height so more rows can render at once","Load the whole table into a collection with ClearCollect during App.OnStart","Rewrite the Items formula to use delegable functions and operators so Dataverse filters and sorts server-side","Change the gallery's TemplateSize to a smaller value"],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Delegation pushes the filter and sort work to Dataverse so large tables are queried server-side; using delegable functions and operators removes the warning and returns all matching rows instead of just the local record cap.","source":{"title":"Build a connected canvas app from Dataverse — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/maker/canvas-apps/how-to/build-connected-dataverse"}},{"id":"gen-56","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"For queries that cannot be delegated, a canvas app only processes a limited number of records locally. What is the maximum value a maker can set for the non-delegable data row limit in the app's settings?","options":["2000","500","5000","50000"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The non-delegable data row limit defaults to 500 and can be raised to a maximum of 2000; beyond that, delegation must be used because the app will not process more rows locally.","source":{"title":"Create a canvas app from scratch using Dataverse — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/maker/canvas-apps/data-platform-create-app-scratch"}},{"id":"gen-57","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker wants a gallery to filter its records as the user types account names into a text-input search box. Which approach on the gallery's Items property achieves this?","options":["Set the gallery's Default property to the search box text","Add an OnSelect formula that calls Refresh on the data source","Bind the gallery to a static collection created once in App.OnStart","Set Items to Search(Accounts, SearchBox.Text, \"name\") so the list narrows to matching rows"],"correct":[3],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The Search function (or a delegable Filter) evaluated against the search box's Text in the gallery's Items property narrows the displayed records live as the user types.","source":{"title":"Build a connected canvas app from Dataverse — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/maker/canvas-apps/how-to/build-connected-dataverse"}},{"id":"gen-58","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"While improving keyboard accessibility, a maker wants the tab order of controls on a screen to follow their logical layout. What is the recommended way to set the TabIndex property?","options":["Give every control a unique positive TabIndex counting up from 1","Leave TabIndex at 0 so focus follows the default order, and avoid positive values that create hard-to-maintain custom sequences","Set TabIndex to -1 on all interactive controls","Set TabIndex to the same positive number for every control"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A TabIndex of 0 lets a control participate in the default tab order; positive values force an explicit order that is fragile and hard to maintain, so the accessibility guidance is to keep TabIndex at 0 where possible.","source":{"title":"Create accessible canvas apps — Accessibility checker — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/maker/canvas-apps/accessibility-checker"}},{"id":"gen-59","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"The Accessibility checker groups the issues it finds by severity. Which grouping represents problems most likely to make the app unusable for people who rely on assistive technology and should be fixed first?","options":["Errors","Tips","Warnings","Suggestions"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The checker reports Errors, Warnings, and Tips; Errors are the highest-severity issues that most directly prevent people using assistive technology from using the app, so they take priority.","source":{"title":"Create accessible canvas apps — Accessibility checker — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/maker/canvas-apps/accessibility-checker"}},{"id":"gen-60","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A canvas app must display well on both phones and tablets by resizing its layout to the available screen. Which first step lets the maker build a responsive design using layout containers?","options":["Set the screen's Fill property to a gradient","Create a separate app for each device form factor","In the app's Display settings, turn off Scale to fit (and Lock aspect ratio) so the screen size adapts to the device","Disable the App.OnStart property"],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Turning off Scale to fit (which also frees Lock aspect ratio) makes the app fill the actual device window, which is the foundation for responsive layouts built with containers and relative sizing formulas.","source":{"title":"Build a connected canvas app from Dataverse — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/maker/canvas-apps/how-to/build-connected-dataverse"}},{"id":"gen-61","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An app's App.OnStart makes several independent calls to different data sources, and it loads slowly because the calls run one after another. Which change most improves startup performance?","options":["Move every data call into the OnVisible of the first screen instead","Duplicate each call so the data is cached twice","Add a Timer control that delays the calls","Wrap the independent calls in Concurrent so they run in parallel rather than sequentially"],"correct":[3],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The Concurrent function evaluates independent formulas at the same time instead of sequentially, which shortens the time OnStart spends loading data and speeds app start-up.","source":{"title":"Canvas app coding standards — code optimization — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/guidance/coding-guidelines/code-optimization"}},{"id":"gen-62","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A formula references the same expensive lookup expression several times, forcing it to be recalculated each time. Which function evaluates the expression once and reuses the named result within the formula?","options":["With, which defines a named value evaluated once and reused inside its scope","ForAll, which repeats the calculation for every record","Concurrent, which runs the calculation multiple times in parallel","Set, which stores the value in a global variable for the whole app"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"With evaluates its named arguments a single time and makes them available inside its formula, avoiding repeated recalculation of the same expensive expression and improving performance.","source":{"title":"Canvas app coding standards — code optimization — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/guidance/coding-guidelines/code-optimization"}},{"id":"gen-63","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A team maintains a shared component library and needs to move it from a development environment to test and production while following healthy ALM. What is the correct approach?","options":["Email the .msapp file and re-import it manually in each environment","Copy and paste the components between apps in each environment","Add the component library to a solution and deploy that solution to the target environments","Recreate the components from scratch in every environment"],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Component libraries are solution-aware; adding the library to a solution and deploying the solution is the supported ALM path for moving reusable components across environments.","source":{"title":"Component library application lifecycle management (ALM) — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/maker/canvas-apps/component-library-alm"}},{"id":"gen-64","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"After a maker publishes an updated version of a component library, how do apps that already consume components from it receive the newer version?","options":["The apps are automatically overwritten with the new components the next time they run","The apps must be rebuilt from scratch to pick up any change","The component library changes never propagate to existing apps","Each consuming app is notified that an update is available, and its maker can choose to apply the update"],"correct":[3],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Consuming apps are notified when a newer component library version exists, and the app maker decides whether to update to it, so changes are adopted deliberately rather than forced.","source":{"title":"Component library in canvas apps — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/maker/canvas-apps/component-library"}},{"id":"gen-65","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker builds a reusable canvas component that must accept a record from the parent screen and return the user's selection back to that screen. Which component feature makes this two-way data exchange possible?","options":["Named formulas defined in App.Formulas","Custom input and output properties defined on the component","The component's Fill and Height properties","A global variable set inside App.OnStart"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Custom properties let a component expose input properties (data passed in from the host) and output properties (values passed back out), which is how a component communicates with the screen that hosts it.","source":{"title":"Component library in canvas apps — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/maker/canvas-apps/component-library"}},{"id":"gen-66","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A value in a canvas app only needs to be available and updated on a single screen. Which technique best fits this scope?","options":["Use UpdateContext to set a context variable, because its scope is limited to that one screen","Use Set to create a global variable available to every screen","Use ClearCollect to create an app-wide collection","Define the value as a named formula in App.Formulas"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Context variables created with UpdateContext are scoped to the screen where they are set, which is the appropriate choice when a value does not need to be shared across the whole app.","source":{"title":"App object — variables and collections (Power Fx) — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-platform/power-fx/reference/object-app"}},{"id":"gen-67","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"Each time a screen loads, a maker wants a collection to be emptied and then refilled with the latest rows from a data source in one operation. Which function does this?","options":["Collect, which only appends rows to whatever the collection already contains","Patch, which updates or creates a single record","ClearCollect, which clears the collection and then adds the new rows in a single call","Refresh, which reloads the data source without changing the collection"],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"ClearCollect removes all existing rows from the collection and then collects the new rows in one step, which is the standard way to fully refresh a collection.","source":{"title":"App object — variables and collections (Power Fx) — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-platform/power-fx/reference/object-app"}},{"id":"gen-68","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"Which statement about a named formula defined in the App.Formulas property is correct?","options":["It can only be referenced from the same screen where it is defined","It recalculates automatically whenever its input values change and cannot be imperatively reassigned with Set","It runs once during App.OnStart and then holds a fixed value for the session","It must be manually refreshed by calling a Refresh function"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Named formulas are declarative: their value is always kept current as their dependencies change, and unlike variables they are immutable, so you cannot overwrite them with Set.","source":{"title":"App object — named formulas (Power Fx) — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-platform/power-fx/reference/object-app"}},{"id":"gen-69","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A Patch that saves a record may occasionally fail, and the maker wants to detect that failure right where the Patch runs and provide fallback logic. Which function should wrap the Patch?","options":["IsBlank, to check whether the record is empty before saving","Notify, to show a message regardless of the outcome","Concurrent, to run the Patch alongside other calls","IfError, to detect the failure inline and run alternative logic or return a fallback value"],"correct":[3],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"IfError evaluates its first argument and, if that produces an error, runs the replacement logic instead, letting the maker handle a failing Patch inline rather than relying only on the app's global error handler.","source":{"title":"App object — error handling (Power Fx) — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-platform/power-fx/reference/object-app"}},{"id":"gen-70","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker wants users to chat with a published Copilot Studio agent directly inside a canvas app screen. What is the correct way to embed it?","options":["Insert the Copilot Studio agent control from the Insert pane and configure it to point at the published agent","Add an HTML text control and paste the agent's script into it","Rebuild the agent's dialogue using standard Label and Button controls","Place a Timer control that periodically calls the agent"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Power Apps provides a Copilot Studio agent (chatbot) control that you insert onto a screen and connect to a published agent, giving app users an in-app conversational experience without rebuilding the dialogue.","source":{"title":"Add a Copilot Studio agent to a canvas app — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/maker/canvas-apps/add-custom-copilot"}},{"id":"gen-71","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"Before releasing a canvas app, a maker wants to author repeatable UI test cases that click controls and assert expected results, grouped for easy reruns. Which capability supports this?","options":["Power Apps Monitor, which passively logs runtime events","The Accessibility checker, which reports accessibility issues","Test Studio, which lets you record, author, and play back test cases organized into test suites","The Formula bar's IntelliSense checker"],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Test Studio is the built-in tool for authoring and running automated UI tests for canvas apps, where individual test cases are grouped into test suites and can be replayed to catch regressions.","source":{"title":"Testing and monitoring canvas apps — coding standards — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/guidance/coding-guidelines/monitoring-testing"}},{"id":"gen-72","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"Users report intermittent data errors in a published canvas app that the maker cannot reproduce in the studio. How can Monitor help diagnose the problem in the live app?","options":["It automatically rewrites the failing formulas to fix them","The maker can connect Monitor to the published app to capture a live trace of events, formulas, and network calls, then inspect the failing operation and its response","It replaces the need for error handling in the app's formulas","It only works while editing the app in Power Apps Studio and cannot observe a published app"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Monitor can attach to a published app session and record a detailed, timed trace of each event and network request, letting the maker reproduce the issue with a user and examine the exact operation and error response.","source":{"title":"Monitor to debug canvas apps — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/power-platform/power-apps/create-use-apps/monitor-debugging-canvas-apps"}},{"id":"gen-73","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker adds the SharePoint connector to a cloud flow so it can react to list changes and create files. In Power Automate, what does a connector provide?","options":["A premium licensing tier required to publish the flow","A set of prebuilt triggers and actions for interacting with a specific service","A visual chart summarizing the flow's run history","A Dataverse security role that grants access to the flow"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A connector is a proxy for a service (SharePoint, Dataverse, Outlook, and so on) that exposes prebuilt triggers and actions you use to build the flow. It is not a license tier, a run-history chart, or a security role.","source":{"title":"Get started with Power Automate","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-automate/getting-started"}},{"id":"gen-74","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"You need a cloud flow that runs automatically every weekday at 8:00 AM with no user interaction. Which type of cloud flow should you create?","options":["An instant flow","An automated flow triggered by a data event","A scheduled flow","A business process flow"],"correct":[2],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A scheduled flow runs on a recurrence you define, such as every weekday at a set time. Instant flows are started on demand, automated flows respond to an event, and a business process flow is not a cloud flow.","source":{"title":"Get started with Power Automate","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-automate/getting-started"}},{"id":"gen-75","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker wants users to start a flow on demand by tapping a button in the Power Automate mobile app or from within a canvas app. Which type of flow supports this?","options":["An instant (manually triggered) flow","A scheduled flow","An automated flow bound to a Dataverse row event","A business rule"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Instant flows are started manually by a user, for example from a button in the mobile app or from a control in a canvas app. Scheduled and automated flows start on a schedule or an event, and a business rule runs on a table, not on demand.","source":{"title":"Get started with Power Automate","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-automate/getting-started"}},{"id":"gen-76","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"You want a cloud flow to run whenever a new row is created in a Dataverse table. Which trigger should you use?","options":["Recurrence","Manually trigger a flow","When an HTTP request is received","When a row is added, modified, or deleted"],"correct":[3],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The Dataverse trigger 'When a row is added, modified, or deleted' fires on row events; set the change type to Added to run on creation. Recurrence runs on a schedule, Manually trigger a flow needs a user, and HTTP request waits for a call.","source":{"title":"Create a cloud flow with Microsoft Dataverse","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-automate/connection-cds"}},{"id":"gen-77","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A flow must start each time a new item is added to a SharePoint list. Which trigger meets this need?","options":["When a row is added, modified, or deleted","When an item is created","Recurrence","For a selected item"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The SharePoint trigger 'When an item is created' runs automatically each time a new list item is added. 'When a row is added...' is the Dataverse trigger, Recurrence is scheduled, and 'For a selected item' is a manual trigger a user runs on a chosen item.","source":{"title":"Trigger flows on a SharePoint library","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-automate/trigger-sharepoint-library"}},{"id":"gen-78","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"You add a trigger condition to a cloud flow to limit when it runs. What must the trigger condition expression evaluate to for the flow to start?","options":["true","A non-empty string value","The number of rows returned by the trigger","Approve"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A trigger condition is a Boolean expression evaluated before the run; the flow starts only when it returns true, which prevents unnecessary runs. It does not return strings, counts, or approval responses.","source":{"title":"Customize your triggers with conditions","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-automate/customize-triggers"}},{"id":"gen-79","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An expense approval must be granted by all three assigned managers before the flow continues. Which approval type should you choose?","options":["Approve/Reject - First to respond","Custom Responses - Wait for one response","Approve/Reject - Everyone must approve","Basic, with no type selected"],"correct":[2],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"'Approve/Reject - Everyone must approve' requires every assigned approver to respond before the request completes. First to respond finishes on a single response, and the custom and 'no type' options do not enforce unanimous approval.","source":{"title":"Get started with Power Automate approvals","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-automate/get-started-approvals"}},{"id":"gen-80","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An approver receives an approval request but will be away and wants a colleague to handle it. Which built-in approvals capability lets them do this without editing the flow?","options":["Trigger conditions","Concurrency control","Configure run after","Reassign the approval to another person"],"correct":[3],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Approvers can reassign an approval request to someone else directly from the approval, so the request is handled without changing the flow. Trigger conditions, concurrency control, and configure run after address unrelated flow behaviors.","source":{"title":"Get started with Power Automate approvals","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-automate/get-started-approvals"}},{"id":"gen-81","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A flow must let approvers choose from options other than Approve and Reject, such as 'Ship now', 'Ship later', or 'Cancel'. Which approval type supports this?","options":["Approve/Reject - Everyone must approve","Custom Responses","Approve/Reject - First to respond","Start and wait for an approval with no type"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The Custom Responses approval type lets you define your own response options instead of the default Approve/Reject choices. The Approve/Reject types only offer Approve or Reject.","source":{"title":"Get started with Power Automate approvals","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-automate/get-started-approvals"}},{"id":"gen-82","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"You want to send an approval request now, run other actions while it is pending, and then wait for the response later in the flow. Which pair of actions supports this pattern?","options":["'Create an approval' now, followed later by 'Wait for an approval'","Two 'Start and wait for an approval' actions in sequence","'Get response details' followed by 'Create an approval'","A Condition action followed by 'Post a message'"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Splitting the approval into 'Create an approval' and a later 'Wait for an approval' lets the flow send the request, continue with other work, and pause for the response only when needed. 'Start and wait for an approval' does both at once and cannot be decoupled.","source":{"title":"Wait for an approval in Power Automate","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-automate/wait-for-approvals"}},{"id":"gen-83","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"After a Condition action evaluates whether an order total exceeds 1000, where do you place the actions that should run only when the total is over 1000?","options":["Inside a Do until loop","In the trigger condition","In the 'If yes' branch of the Condition","In a separate scheduled flow"],"correct":[2],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A Condition action splits the flow into an 'If yes' branch that runs when the condition is true and an 'If no' branch for when it is false. Actions for the over-1000 case go in the 'If yes' branch.","source":{"title":"Add a condition to a cloud flow","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-automate/multi-step-logic-flow"}},{"id":"gen-84","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"You need a single Condition that passes only when the status is 'Open' AND the priority is 'High', combining multiple checks. How do you build this?","options":["Add a trigger condition to the Recurrence trigger","Wrap the checks in an Apply to each action","Create a rollup column on the table","Add multiple rows to the Condition and choose the AND grouping (or edit in advanced mode)"],"correct":[3],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A Condition can contain multiple rows combined with AND or OR grouping, and advanced mode lets you write the combined expression directly. Trigger conditions, loops, and rollup columns do not build a single grouped condition.","source":{"title":"Use expressions in conditions in Power Automate","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-automate/use-expressions-in-conditions"}},{"id":"gen-85","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A flow retrieves a list of Dataverse rows and must send an email for each row returned. Which action iterates over the collection?","options":["Do until","Apply to each","Condition","Scope"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Apply to each loops over an array or collection, running its contained actions once per item. Do until repeats until a condition is met, Condition branches once, and Scope only groups actions.","source":{"title":"Use the Apply to each loop in Power Automate","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-automate/apply-to-each"}},{"id":"gen-86","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"You need a flow to keep polling a record's status and repeat a set of actions until the status becomes 'Complete'. Which loop should you use?","options":["Do until","Apply to each","Switch","The Recurrence trigger"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The Do until loop repeats its actions until a specified condition becomes true, which fits polling until the status is 'Complete'. Apply to each iterates a known collection, Switch branches on a value, and Recurrence is a trigger, not a loop.","source":{"title":"Create a Do until loop in Power Automate","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-automate/create-loop-with-do-until"}},{"id":"gen-87","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An Apply to each loop processes items one at a time, but you need it to process multiple items in parallel so the flow finishes faster. Which setting do you change?","options":["The flow's trigger condition","The flow's environment type","The approval type used in the loop","Turn on concurrency control and set the degree of parallelism"],"correct":[3],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"In the Apply to each settings you can enable concurrency control and set the degree of parallelism so iterations run in parallel instead of sequentially. Trigger conditions, environment type, and approval type do not control loop parallelism.","source":{"title":"Use the Apply to each loop in Power Automate","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-automate/apply-to-each"}},{"id":"gen-88","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"You want a 'send failure alert' action to run only when the previous action fails or times out. Which setting achieves this?","options":["Add a trigger condition to the flow","Insert a Terminate action before the alert","Set Configure run after on the alert action to run when the prior action has failed or timed out","Increase the loop's degree of parallelism"],"correct":[2],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Configure run after lets you specify which prior-step outcomes (successful, failed, skipped, timed out) allow an action to run, so the alert runs only on failure or timeout. This is the standard pattern for catching errors in a flow.","source":{"title":"Handle errors and set up notifications in Power Automate","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-automate/error-handling"}},{"id":"gen-89","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"You want to group several actions for try/catch-style error handling, then stop the flow and mark it as Failed when something goes wrong. Which two features help?","options":["The Recurrence trigger and an Apply to each loop","The Scope action to group steps and the Terminate action to end the run with a status","Trigger conditions and filter columns","Custom Responses and Reassign"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A Scope groups related actions so you can apply run-after handling to the whole block, and the Terminate action stops the flow and sets its status, such as Failed. The other pairs address triggering, filtering, and approvals, not error handling.","source":{"title":"Handle errors and set up notifications in Power Automate","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-automate/error-handling"}},{"id":"gen-90","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"One action in a flow keeps failing in production. To diagnose it, where do you look to see each action's inputs, outputs, and error message for a specific run?","options":["The flow's run history, by opening the failed run and expanding the action","The Dataverse security roles page","The solution layers dialog","The Power Platform admin center's environment list"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Run history lists each run; opening a run and expanding an action shows its inputs, outputs, and any error, which is how you troubleshoot a failing step. Security roles, solution layers, and the environment list do not show per-action run details.","source":{"title":"Create approval flows with attachments — test and monitor your flow","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-automate/approval-attachments"}},{"id":"gen-91","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker needs a custom prompt that summarizes long customer feedback emails into three bullet points. They want the quickest starting point that already contains sample instructions they can adapt. In AI Hub, what should they choose when creating the prompt?","options":["Create a prompt column on the feedback table","Start from a prompt template and modify its instructions","Build a business rule that concatenates the feedback","Import an AI Builder prebuilt invoice-processing model"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"AI Hub prompt templates give makers a pre-written, editable prompt for common tasks such as summarization, which is faster than authoring instructions from scratch.","source":{"title":"Create a custom prompt — Microsoft Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/create-custom-prompt"}},{"id":"gen-92","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"Your organization has a highly specific extraction task that none of the AI Hub prompt templates cover. You want full control to author the model instructions yourself. Which option should you select when creating the prompt in AI Hub?","options":["Convert an existing prebuilt prompt to editable mode","Add a prompt column so the model runs on each row","Create the prompt from blank and write your own instructions","Use a business process flow to guide the extraction"],"correct":[2],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Building a custom prompt from blank lets the maker write the full instruction text and configure inputs when no template fits the scenario.","source":{"title":"Create a custom prompt — Microsoft Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/create-custom-prompt"}},{"id":"gen-93","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"You are authoring a custom prompt that should classify any support ticket text a user provides at run time, rather than a fixed sentence baked into the instructions. What must you add to the prompt so the ticket text can be supplied when the prompt is called?","options":["An input that you reference as a placeholder inside the instructions","A rollup column that aggregates the ticket text","A separate cloud flow trigger for each ticket","A Dataverse relationship to the tickets table"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Adding an input creates a named placeholder you insert into the prompt instructions; the value is passed in each time the prompt runs, making the prompt reusable with dynamic data.","source":{"title":"Create a custom prompt — Microsoft Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/create-custom-prompt"}},{"id":"gen-94","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker added an input named CustomerName to a custom prompt but the model ignores it and never personalizes the output. What is the most likely cause?","options":["Inputs are only supported in prebuilt prompts, not custom ones","The prompt must be published as a solution before inputs work","AI Builder credits must be purchased before inputs are honored","The input placeholder was never inserted into the instruction text"],"correct":[3],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Defining an input is not enough; the maker must place the input's placeholder token within the prompt instructions so the model actually uses the supplied value.","source":{"title":"Create a custom prompt — Microsoft Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/create-custom-prompt"}},{"id":"gen-95","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"During testing, a custom prompt produces short, deterministic answers, but the team wants more varied, creative phrasing for marketing copy. Where in AI Hub should the maker make this adjustment?","options":["Add a new input for each variation of the copy","Recreate the prompt as an AI Builder prebuilt model","In the prompt settings, adjust the model configuration such as the GPT model and response behavior","Move the prompt logic into a Power Fx named formula"],"correct":[2],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Prompt settings let makers customize the underlying model and its behavior, which controls how creative or deterministic the generated responses are.","source":{"title":"Create a custom prompt — Microsoft Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/create-custom-prompt"}},{"id":"gen-96","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A prompt must answer questions using your company's internal product documentation rather than the model's general training data. In AI Hub, what should you configure so the prompt is grounded on that content?","options":["Add knowledge so the prompt is grounded on your own data sources","Increase the model's creativity setting to allow more detail","Add more text inputs so users can paste documentation manually","Convert the prompt into a business rule on the product table"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Adding knowledge grounds the prompt on your own data, such as Dataverse tables or files, so responses reflect your organization's content instead of only the model's general knowledge.","source":{"title":"Use your own data to ground a prompt — Microsoft Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/use-your-own-prompt-data"}},{"id":"gen-97","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"You built and saved a custom prompt in AI Hub and now want to call it from a canvas app so a button generates a summary from a text input control. Which approach lets the canvas app invoke the prompt?","options":["Add the prompt as a business process flow stage","Reference the saved AI Builder prompt as a model and call it with Power Fx","Embed the prompt instructions directly in the button's OnSelect as text","Create a Dataverse view that runs the prompt on each record"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A saved custom prompt becomes an AI Builder model you can add to a canvas app and invoke with Power Fx, passing the input control's value and reading the returned response.","source":{"title":"Use a custom prompt in an app — AI Builder","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/ai-builder/use-a-custom-prompt-in-app"}},{"id":"gen-98","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A cloud flow triggered when a new Dataverse row is created should generate a personalized reply using a custom prompt you built in AI Hub. Which action lets the flow run that prompt?","options":["Use the Dataverse 'Perform a bound action' with no parameters","Add an Approvals action that references the prompt name","Insert a Condition action that evaluates the prompt text","Add the AI Builder action that runs your saved custom prompt"],"correct":[3],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Power Automate exposes an AI Builder action to run a saved custom prompt, letting the flow pass dynamic inputs and use the generated text in later steps.","source":{"title":"Use a custom prompt in a flow — AI Builder","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/ai-builder/use-a-custom-prompt-in-flow"}},{"id":"gen-99","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker wants sentiment scoring on incoming reviews but does not want to author any instructions or manage a model. Which AI Hub option best fits this need?","options":["Build a custom prompt from blank and describe sentiment scoring","Create a prompt column that stores the review sentiment","Use a prebuilt prompt that already performs sentiment analysis","Add a rollup column that averages review ratings"],"correct":[2],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Prebuilt prompts such as sentiment analysis, categorization, and summarization are ready to use with no authoring, ideal when a common task is already covered.","source":{"title":"Prebuilt prompts — AI Builder","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/ai-builder/prebuilt-prompts"}},{"id":"gen-100","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"In a cloud flow you need to summarize a block of text using an out-of-the-box capability, without first building or training anything. What is the most direct way to add this in Power Automate?","options":["Add the AI Builder 'Create text with GPT' prebuilt prompt action and pass the text","Create a new custom prompt from blank and publish it as a solution first","Configure a business rule on the source table to summarize the text","Use a Copilot Studio agent topic to return the summary"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Power Automate offers AI Builder prebuilt prompt actions (for example, GPT-based text generation and summarization) that work immediately without authoring or training a model.","source":{"title":"Use AI Builder in Power Automate","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-automate/use-ai-builder"}},{"id":"gen-101","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"Several flows that call AI Builder prompts suddenly fail with a message that all AI Builder credits have been consumed. What is the appropriate way to restore service?","options":["Recreate each prompt from a template to reset its usage","Move the prompts into an unmanaged solution to bypass metering","Switch every prompt from GPT to a business rule","Provision additional AI Builder credits or capacity for the environment"],"correct":[3],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"AI Builder prompt and model calls consume metered credits; when they run out, adding capacity or credits for the environment is required to resume processing.","source":{"title":"All AI Builder credits have been consumed — Troubleshoot","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/power-platform/ai-builder/all-ai-builder-credits-have-been-consumed"}},{"id":"gen-102","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"Before saving a custom prompt, a maker wants to confirm it returns the right output for a realistic example. What is the recommended way to validate the prompt in AI Hub?","options":["Publish the prompt to production and inspect the first live run","Enter sample input values and run a test to preview the response","Export the prompt to a solution and import it into another environment","Add the prompt to a canvas app screen and select the button repeatedly"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The prompt authoring experience lets makers supply sample input values and test the prompt, previewing the model's output before saving or consuming it.","source":{"title":"Create a custom prompt — Microsoft Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/create-custom-prompt"}},{"id":"gen-103","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A team needs one generative-AI instruction that can be reused identically from both a canvas app and multiple cloud flows. Which AI Hub artifact best meets this reuse requirement?","options":["A prompt column added to a single Dataverse table","A calculated column that concatenates the source fields","A saved custom prompt that both apps and flows can call","A business process flow shared across the apps"],"correct":[2],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A saved custom prompt is a reusable AI Builder model that can be consumed from canvas apps and from Power Automate flows, unlike a prompt column which is tied to one table.","source":{"title":"Use a custom prompt in an app — AI Builder","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/ai-builder/use-a-custom-prompt-in-app"}},{"id":"gen-104","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A custom prompt is expected to return results the app can parse into fields, but it currently returns free-form paragraphs. What is the most effective change to the prompt itself?","options":["Specify the desired output format in the prompt instructions","Lower the AI Builder credit allocation for the environment","Remove all inputs so the model has less to process","Convert the prompt into a rollup column"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Instructing the model on the exact output structure (for example, labeled fields or a specific format) within the prompt text is the standard way to get consistently parseable responses.","source":{"title":"Create a custom prompt — Microsoft Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/create-custom-prompt"}},{"id":"gen-105","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"While customizing a prompt's settings, a maker sees an option to select which GPT model powers the prompt. What is the practical impact of choosing a different model?","options":["It changes the Dataverse table the prompt writes to","It automatically translates the prompt into other languages","It converts the prompt into a prebuilt model shared org-wide","It can affect the prompt's response quality, capability, and cost"],"correct":[3],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Selecting the underlying model in prompt settings influences the capability, quality, and consumption cost of the responses the prompt generates.","source":{"title":"Create a custom prompt — Microsoft Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/create-custom-prompt"}},{"id":"gen-106","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker finished a custom prompt and wants colleagues in the same environment to reuse it in their own apps and flows. What is the correct way to make the prompt available to them?","options":["Paste the prompt instructions into a shared spreadsheet","Share the AI Builder prompt so other makers can consume it","Add the prompt as a public view on a Dataverse table","Convert the prompt to a business rule that runs for everyone"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A custom prompt is an AI Builder object that can be shared with other makers, letting them consume the same prompt from their apps and flows.","source":{"title":"Use a custom prompt in a flow — AI Builder","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/ai-builder/use-a-custom-prompt-in-flow"}},{"id":"gen-107","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"In a canvas app, a maker wants to generate a draft reply from user-entered text using Power Fx, leveraging an out-of-the-box generative capability rather than building a model. Which approach is appropriate?","options":["Store the text in a collection and hope the app summarizes it","Add a business process flow to the app's screen","Use a Power Fx AI function that calls an AI Builder prebuilt capability","Create a rollup column to aggregate the entered text"],"correct":[2],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Canvas apps can consume AI Builder capabilities directly through Power Fx AI functions, letting a formula generate or transform text from a control's value without training a model.","source":{"title":"Use a custom prompt in an app — AI Builder","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/ai-builder/use-a-custom-prompt-in-app"}},{"id":"gen-108","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"When adding knowledge to ground a custom prompt on organizational content, which of the following is a valid type of source the prompt can use?","options":["Dataverse tables and uploaded files containing your content","The device's local clipboard history","A canvas app's ephemeral variables and collections","The environment's ALM solution layering order"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Grounding a prompt with knowledge draws on your own data such as Dataverse tables and files, so responses are based on that content rather than only the model's general training.","source":{"title":"Use your own data to ground a prompt — Microsoft Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/use-your-own-prompt-data"}},{"id":"gen-109","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker needs to make the Credit Limit column required on an Account form only when the Category column equals 'Preferred', without writing any code. Which Dataverse feature should they use?","options":["A business rule with a condition that sets the requirement level","A rollup column on the Account table","A calculated column with an IF formula","A canvas app OnStart formula"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"sub":"3.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Business rules can evaluate a condition and then set a column's requirement level (business required or not) with no code, which is exactly this scenario. Rollup and calculated columns compute values but cannot change a form's requirement level.","source":{"title":"Dataverse business rules and recommendations — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/data-platform/processes"}},{"id":"gen-110","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"You build a business rule and set its scope to 'Entity' (Table) rather than to a specific form. What is the effect of this choice?","options":["The rule runs only in the classic web client","The rule applies only to the main form it was created from","The rule applies to all of the table's forms and also runs on the server, so it is enforced regardless of which app writes the data","The rule is disabled until a plug-in registers it"],"correct":[2],"skill":3,"sub":"3.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Entity (table) scope enforces the rule across all forms and at the server level, so the logic applies even for data changes made outside a specific form. Form-level scope limits the rule to a single form.","source":{"title":"Dataverse business rules — scope — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/data-platform/processes"}},{"id":"gen-111","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A business rule uses the 'Show error message' action and also 'Show/Hide columns' and 'Enable/Disable columns' actions. A maker reuses the same rule in a canvas app and finds some behavior missing. Why?","options":["Canvas apps ignore all business rules","The rule must be converted to a plug-in first","Business rules only run inside cloud flows","Some actions such as show/hide and enable/disable columns are supported only in model-driven apps, while canvas apps honor a subset like setting values and validation"],"correct":[3],"skill":3,"sub":"3.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Business rules run in both app types, but visibility and enable/disable actions are model-driven-only; canvas apps support a subset such as setting column values, requirement level, and validation. That is why show/hide behavior does not appear in the canvas app.","source":{"title":"Dataverse business rules — client support — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/data-platform/processes"}},{"id":"gen-112","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker wants a form to suggest a discount value to the user but still let them choose whether to accept it. Which business rule capability fits best?","options":["A rollup column","A business recommendation, which prompts the user with a suggested action they can apply or dismiss","A required-level validation that blocks saving","A business process flow stage gate"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"sub":"3.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A business recommendation displays a suggestion the user can apply or ignore, which matches a non-mandatory suggested discount. Validation would block the save rather than suggest, and rollups only aggregate related rows.","source":{"title":"Dataverse business rules and recommendations — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/data-platform/processes"}},{"id":"gen-113","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A sales onboarding process must move a single lead record through Qualify, Develop, and Close phases that live on different Dataverse tables. Which feature models this guided, cross-table journey?","options":["A business process flow, which can span up to five tables in ordered stages","A rollup column chain","A single business rule set to Entity scope","A calculated column referencing the parent"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"sub":"3.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A business process flow guides users through ordered stages and can span up to five tables, which fits a lead progressing across related tables. Business rules and computed columns do not provide a multi-stage guided experience.","source":{"title":"Create a business process flow — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/dynamics365/customerengagement/on-premises/customize/create-business-process-flow"}},{"id":"gen-114","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An admin activates several business process flows for the same table. What determines which flow a record starts with by default?","options":["The alphabetical name of the flow","The most recently modified flow always wins","The process order (ranked list) of the activated flows, combined with the user's security-role access to them","Only one business process flow is ever allowed per table"],"correct":[2],"skill":3,"sub":"3.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A table can have multiple activated business process flows (up to ten), and the ordered process list plus the user's role access decide the default flow applied to a record. Name and modified date do not drive the default.","source":{"title":"Create a business process flow — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-automate/create-business-process-flow"}},{"id":"gen-115","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker finishes designing a business process flow but users report they cannot see or use it. What two steps most likely remain?","options":["Convert the table to virtual and republish","Activate the flow and grant the relevant security roles access to it","Add a rollup column and refresh it","Export the solution as managed"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"sub":"3.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A newly authored business process flow is a draft until it is activated, and users additionally need the appropriate security role access to the flow. Without activation and role access, it stays invisible to users.","source":{"title":"Create a business process flow — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-automate/create-business-process-flow"}},{"id":"gen-116","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A team needs a background automation that sends an email and updates records whenever an order is created, with no on-screen guidance for the user. Which tool is the better fit than a business process flow?","options":["A rollup column","A calculated column","A business rule with recommendation actions","A cloud flow (Power Automate) triggered on create"],"correct":[3],"skill":3,"sub":"3.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A business process flow provides a visual, staged guide for users, whereas silent background automation on create is better served by a cloud flow. The other options compute or suggest but do not orchestrate background actions.","source":{"title":"Create a business process flow — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-automate/create-business-process-flow"}},{"id":"gen-117","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"When designing a business process flow, what is the maximum number of tables a single flow can span?","options":["Up to five tables","Exactly one table","Up to fifty tables","There is no limit"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"sub":"3.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A business process flow can span up to five tables, letting stages move a process across related tables. It is not limited to one table, nor is it unlimited.","source":{"title":"Create a business process flow — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/dynamics365/customerengagement/on-premises/customize/create-business-process-flow"}},{"id":"gen-118","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker adds a rollup column that sums related invoice amounts, but a newly added invoice's value does not appear on the parent immediately. What explains this?","options":["Rollup columns never update after creation","Rollup columns are recalculated by an asynchronous system job on a schedule, so values update periodically rather than instantly","The rollup requires a plug-in to compute","Rollups only work in canvas apps"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"sub":"3.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Rollup columns are recalculated by a scheduled asynchronous system job, so aggregated values reflect changes after the job runs rather than in real time. A manual recalculate can be triggered from the column if needed.","source":{"title":"Define rollup columns in Dataverse — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/data-platform/define-rollup-fields"}},{"id":"gen-119","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker tries to build a rollup column that aggregates values from another rollup column on the related table. What will they find?","options":["It works and updates instantly","It is allowed only in managed solutions","A rollup column cannot reference another rollup column, and it aggregates over a one-to-many relationship","Rollups can only reference many-to-many relationships"],"correct":[2],"skill":3,"sub":"3.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Rollup columns aggregate related rows over a one-to-many relationship and cannot chain by referencing another rollup column. This is a documented restriction of rollup fields.","source":{"title":"Define rollup columns in Dataverse — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/data-platform/define-rollup-fields"}},{"id":"gen-120","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A finance user needs an up-to-the-second total on a parent record right after editing a child row, rather than waiting for the scheduled job. What can they do with a rollup column?","options":["Nothing — rollups cannot be refreshed manually","Convert the rollup to a many-to-many aggregate","Delete and recreate the column each time","Use the manual recalculate (refresh) action on the rollup column to force an immediate recalculation"],"correct":[3],"skill":3,"sub":"3.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Besides the scheduled asynchronous job, a rollup column exposes a manual recalculate action so a user can force an on-demand refresh. This is the supported way to get an immediate value between scheduled runs.","source":{"title":"Define rollup columns in Dataverse — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/data-platform/define-rollup-fields"}},{"id":"gen-121","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker needs a Full Name column that concatenates First and Last name and is always current when a row is retrieved, without storing a physical value. Which column type fits?","options":["A calculated column, which derives its value from a formula on the current row at retrieval time","A rollup column","A whole-number column with a default","A prompt column"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"sub":"3.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A calculated column derives its value from a formula on the current row (and related parent) and is computed when the data is retrieved, not stored. Rollups aggregate related rows, and neither number nor prompt columns concatenate on the fly.","source":{"title":"Create and edit columns — calculated columns — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/data-platform/create-edit-field-portal"}},{"id":"gen-122","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"How does a Dataverse formula column differ from a classic calculated column?","options":["A formula column can only aggregate related rows","A formula column uses Power Fx expressions to compute its value in real time, offering a more modern authoring experience than the classic calculated column","A formula column stores its value and never recalculates","A formula column runs only inside cloud flows"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"sub":"3.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Formula columns use Power Fx to compute values in real time and are the modern alternative to classic calculated columns. Both compute on the current row rather than aggregating related rows the way rollups do.","source":{"title":"Power Fx formula reference — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-platform/power-fx/formula-reference-overview"}},{"id":"gen-123","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker must show, on each Account, the number of related open Cases. Which column type is correct, and why not a formula/calculated column?","options":["A calculated column, because it can count child rows in real time","A prompt column, because it summarizes rows","A rollup column, because counting or summing values across related child rows requires aggregation, which calculated and formula columns cannot do","A choice column, because open Cases are a status"],"correct":[2],"skill":3,"sub":"3.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Counting or summing across related child rows is aggregation, which is the job of a rollup column; calculated and formula columns operate on the current row and cannot aggregate related rows. That is why a rollup is required here.","source":{"title":"Define rollup columns in Dataverse — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/data-platform/define-rollup-fields"}},{"id":"gen-124","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"Which statement about calculated columns in Dataverse is correct?","options":["They physically store their result in the table","They can aggregate values across many-to-many relationships","They are editable directly by users on the form","They are read-only, computed at retrieval time from a formula, and are not stored as a physical value"],"correct":[3],"skill":3,"sub":"3.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Calculated columns are read-only and computed on demand from their formula when the record is retrieved; they are not physically stored and cannot aggregate related rows. Aggregation is a rollup capability.","source":{"title":"Create and edit columns — calculated columns — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/data-platform/create-edit-field-portal"}},{"id":"gen-125","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A requirement states: 'When Order Type is International, the Customs Code column must become mandatory and a warning must appear if it is left blank.' Which single low-code feature satisfies both parts?","options":["A business rule that conditionally sets the requirement level and shows a validation error message","A rollup column summing customs codes","A component library with a reusable control","A calculated column returning the customs code"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"sub":"3.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A business rule can, on a condition, both set a column to business required and display an error message, covering the mandatory and warning requirements without code. Rollup, component, and calculated columns cannot enforce requirement levels or show validation errors.","source":{"title":"Dataverse business rules and recommendations — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/data-platform/processes"}},{"id":"gen-126","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An Opportunity record must display the sum of the Amount column across all its related Opportunity Line rows, updated automatically over time. Which choice implements this with the least maintenance?","options":["A cloud flow that recalculates the total on every edit","A business rule set to Entity scope","A rollup column that sums the related line Amounts via the one-to-many relationship","A manually entered number column the user keeps current"],"correct":[2],"skill":3,"sub":"3.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A rollup column natively sums a value across related child rows over a one-to-many relationship and maintains itself via the scheduled recalculation job, requiring no custom automation. A cloud flow or manual column would be more effort to keep accurate.","source":{"title":"Define rollup columns in Dataverse — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-apps/maker/data-platform/define-rollup-fields"}}];
